-- Run in the Supabase SQL editor (requires write access, i.e. run as yourself in the dashboard).

insert into public.questions
  (template_id, section, domain, skill, difficulty, question_text, choices, correct_answer, explanation, distractor_analysis, quality_score, status)
values
  ($q$RW-001$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$Marine biologist Ayana Elizabeth Johnson argues that solutions to climate change are not ______; the ocean, she notes, offers a wealth of untapped strategies, from restoring coastal wetlands to expanding offshore wind.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["scarce", "abundant", "theoretical", "expensive"]$q$::jsonb, $q$scarce$q$, $q$The clause after the semicolon says the ocean offers 'a wealth of untapped strategies.' For the passage to be logical, the first clause must claim solutions are NOT rare — so 'scarce' fits the 'not ___' structure.$q$, $q${"abundant": "Misreads the 'not ___' construction and picks a word matching the ocean's abundance directly, instead of the word that gets negated by 'not'.", "theoretical": "Assumes the contrast is about untested vs. practical ideas rather than about how plentiful the solutions are.", "expensive": "Imports outside assumptions about climate solutions being costly instead of following the sentence's actual comparison to the ocean's 'wealth of untapped strategies.'"}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-002$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence$q$, 2, $q$A student claims that urban community gardens meaningfully reduce household grocery spending for participating families.

Which finding, if true, would most directly support the student's claim?$q$, $q$["Families in a community-garden program reported spending 18% less on produce over a growing season than comparable non-participating families.", "Community gardens are most often located in densely populated neighborhoods.", "Participants said gardening improved their mood and sense of community.", "The number of community gardens in the city doubled over five years."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Families in a community-garden program reported spending 18% less on produce over a growing season than comparable non-participating families.$q$, $q$The claim is specifically about reduced grocery spending. Only choice A provides quantitative evidence tying garden participation to lower spending on food.$q$, $q${"Community gardens are most often located in densely populated neighborhoods.": "Treats a descriptive fact about location as if it were evidence about spending, with no logical link to the grocery-savings claim.", "Participants said gardening improved their mood and sense of community.": "Confuses an emotional/social benefit with the specific financial claim the question asks about.", "The number of community gardens in the city doubled over five years.": "Mistakes growth in the number of gardens for proof that individual families save money — correlation with no direct connection to spending."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-003$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$By the time the expedition reached the summit ______ nearly every member had run out of supplemental oxygen.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ", ": ", "; ", ""]$q$::jsonb, $q$, $q$, $q$The introductory dependent clause ('By the time...summit') must be separated from the main clause with a comma. A colon or semicolon would incorrectly imply the first part can stand alone.$q$, $q${": ": "Misapplies colon usage, treating the introductory clause as if it introduces a list or explanation rather than recognizing it as a dependent clause needing a comma.", "; ": "Wrongly treats the introductory clause ('By the time...summit') as if it could stand alone as an independent clause.", "": "Fails to separate the introductory dependent clause from the main clause at all, creating a run-on."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-004$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The researchers noted that the migratory patterns of the songbirds ______ significantly over the past decade, likely in response to warming temperatures.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have shifted", "has shifted", "shifting", "to shift"]$q$::jsonb, $q$have shifted$q$, $q$The subject 'patterns' is plural, so it needs the plural verb 'have shifted.' 'Over the past decade' signals present-perfect tense.$q$, $q${"has shifted": "Subject-verb agreement error — agrees the verb with the nearer noun ('songbirds' or 'decade') instead of the actual plural subject 'patterns.'", "shifting": "Drops the auxiliary verb, leaving the sentence without a complete predicate.", "to shift": "Misreads the sentence as needing an infinitive, ignoring the 'over the past decade' cue that calls for present-perfect tense."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-005$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Solar panel efficiency has improved dramatically in the last fifteen years. ______ the cost of installation has fallen by more than 70%, making solar accessible to far more households.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Moreover,", "Nevertheless,", "In contrast,", "For example,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Moreover,$q$, $q$The second sentence adds a second positive development (falling cost) to the first (rising efficiency). 'Moreover' signals addition of a supporting point.$q$, $q${"Nevertheless,": "Assumes a contrast between the two sentences when both actually support the same positive trend.", "In contrast,": "Same misreading of the relationship as oppositional rather than additive.", "For example,": "Treats the second sentence as a specific instance of the first, rather than a second, independent supporting point."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-006$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas$q$, 3, $q$Historians once portrayed the medieval period as a cultural 'dark age,' but recent scholarship emphasizes vibrant networks of trade, translation, and intellectual exchange that spanned continents. Manuscripts preserved and expanded classical knowledge, while universities emerged as centers of rigorous debate.

Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Newer scholarship challenges the view of the medieval period as intellectually stagnant.", "Medieval universities were the first institutions to preserve classical texts.", "Trade networks were the most important feature of medieval life.", "Historians have always agreed about the vibrancy of medieval culture."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Newer scholarship challenges the view of the medieval period as intellectually stagnant.$q$, $q$The passage contrasts the old 'dark age' view with recent scholarship showing intellectual vibrancy. Choice A captures that reframing; the others overstate details or contradict the text.$q$, $q${"Medieval universities were the first institutions to preserve classical texts.": "Overgeneralizes a supporting detail into the main idea, mistaking one example for the passage's overall claim.", "Trade networks were the most important feature of medieval life.": "Elevates a single supporting detail to 'most important,' a ranking the text never makes.", "Historians have always agreed about the vibrancy of medieval culture.": "Contradicts the passage's explicit framing of a shift in view ('Historians once... but recent scholarship...')."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-007$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 3, $q$Though critics initially dismissed the composer's work as ______, later generations recognized its intricate structure and emotional depth, elevating pieces once ignored into staples of the concert repertoire.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["slight", "melodic", "controversial", "revolutionary"]$q$::jsonb, $q$slight$q$, $q$The contrast ('Though...but later recognized...depth') requires an initially negative, dismissive judgment. 'Slight' means trivial or insubstantial, fitting the dismissal that later proved wrong.$q$, $q${"melodic": "Picks a neutral musical descriptor that doesn't carry the dismissive tone the sentence requires.", "controversial": "Close in register but implies active debate rather than the passage's meaning of being dismissed as insignificant.", "revolutionary": "Describes the later recognition, not the critics' initial dismissal — the opposite of what the blank needs."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-008$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The city council approved the new bike lane ______ construction is scheduled to begin in the spring.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ", ", ", " ", " and, "]$q$::jsonb, $q$; $q$, $q$Two independent clauses ('The council approved...' and 'construction is scheduled...') must be joined by a semicolon (or a comma + conjunction). Only the semicolon is offered correctly.$q$, $q${", ": "A comma alone can't join two independent clauses without a conjunction, creating a comma splice.", " ": "Fails to punctuate the boundary at all, creating a run-on sentence.", " and, ": "Misplaces the comma after 'and' instead of before it — the wrong punctuation pattern for joining independent clauses."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-001$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations$q$, 1, $q$If 3x + 7 = 22, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5", "7", "15", "29/3"]$q$::jsonb, $q$5$q$, $q$3x + 7 = 22 → 3x = 15 → x = 5.$q$, $q${"7": "Skips isolating the variable term first and instead divides the full constant 22 by 3, then rounds the result (~7.3) down to 7.", "15": "Correctly finds 3x = 15 but stops there, forgetting the final division by 3.", "29/3": "Sign error — adds 7 instead of subtracting it (3x = 22 + 7 = 29), giving x = 29/3."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-002$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios & proportions$q$, 1, $q$A recipe requires 2 cups of flour for every 3 cups of sugar. If a baker uses 8 cups of flour, how many cups of sugar are needed?$q$, $q$["12", "10", "9", "16"]$q$::jsonb, $q$12$q$, $q$Set up the proportion 2/3 = 8/x. Cross-multiply: 2x = 24, so x = 12 cups of sugar.$q$, $q${"10": "Adds the difference between the ratio numbers (3 − 2 = 1) to the flour amount instead of scaling proportionally.", "9": "Averages or blends the ratio numbers with the flour amount instead of setting up a true proportion.", "16": "Simply doubles the flour amount, treating the sugar requirement as 'twice the flour' instead of applying the 2:3 scale factor."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-003$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, $q$A jacket originally priced at $80 is discounted by 25%. A sales tax of 8% is then applied to the discounted price. What is the final price?$q$, $q$["$64.80", "$60.00", "$66.00", "$62.40"]$q$::jsonb, $q$$64.80$q$, $q$Discounted price: 80 × 0.75 = $60. With tax: 60 × 1.08 = $64.80.$q$, $q${"$60.00": "Applies the 25% discount correctly but forgets to add the 8% sales tax afterward.", "$66.00": "Applies both percentages to the original price separately and adds them, instead of applying tax to the already-discounted price.", "$62.40": "Applies the tax rate to the discount amount instead of to the discounted price ($80 × 0.75 = $60 is right, but then miscombines the 8%)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-004$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of equations$q$, 2, $q$If 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4", "3", "5", "6"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4$q$, $q$Add the equations: (2x + y) + (x − y) = 11 + 1 → 3x = 12 → x = 4.$q$, $q${"3": "Sign error when substituting y = x − 1 into the other equation (drops the negative), landing near x ≈ 3.3 and rounding down.", "5": "Subtracts the two equations instead of adding them, losing track of the y-terms and arriving at the wrong value for x.", "6": "Correctly reaches 3x = 12 but divides by 2 instead of 3."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-005$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratics$q$, 2, $q$The expression x² − 5x + 6 can be factored as (x − a)(x − b). What is the value of a + b?$q$, $q$["5", "6", "1", "−5"]$q$::jsonb, $q$5$q$, $q$x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3), so a and b are 2 and 3. Their sum is 5 (which equals the coefficient of the middle term with sign flipped).$q$, $q${"6": "Reports the product ab (2 × 3 = 6, the constant term) instead of the sum a + b.", "1": "Reports the difference b − a instead of the sum.", "−5": "Keeps the sign from the original −5x coefficient instead of flipping it, since a and b satisfy x − a and x − b with a + b = 5, not −5."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-006$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, $q$A line passes through the points (1, 4) and (3, 10). What is the slope of the line?$q$, $q$["3", "2", "6", "1/3"]$q$::jsonb, $q$3$q$, $q$Slope = (10 − 4) / (3 − 1) = 6 / 2 = 3.$q$, $q${"2": "Miscounts the run, using 3 (the x-coordinate of the second point) instead of the actual change in x (3 − 1 = 2).", "6": "Reports the rise (10 − 4 = 6) without dividing by the run, forgetting slope is rise ÷ run.", "1/3": "Inverts the slope formula, computing run ÷ rise instead of rise ÷ run."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-007$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponents$q$, 2, $q$If 2^(x+1) = 32, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4", "5", "3", "6"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4$q$, $q$32 = 2^5, so x + 1 = 5, giving x = 4.$q$, $q${"5": "Solves 2^x = 32 (x = 5) but forgets to account for the '+1' in the exponent.", "3": "Miscounts the powers of 2 (2⁵ = 32 mistaken for 2⁴), then still subtracts 1.", "6": "Treats 32 as 2⁶ instead of 2⁵, an off-by-one error in the power of 2."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-008$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Geometry$q$, 2, $q$A right triangle has legs of length 6 and 8. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["10", "14", "48", "√28"]$q$::jsonb, $q$10$q$, $q$By the Pythagorean theorem: √(6² + 8²) = √(36 + 64) = √100 = 10.$q$, $q${"14": "Adds the two legs directly (6 + 8 = 14) instead of applying the Pythagorean theorem.", "48": "Multiplies the legs (6 × 8 = 48) instead of squaring and summing them.", "√28": "Subtracts the squares instead of adding them (8² − 6² = 28) before taking the square root."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-009$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Statistics$q$, 2, $q$The list of numbers 4, 8, 8, 10, 15 has what median?$q$, $q$["8", "9", "10", "8.5"]$q$::jsonb, $q$8$q$, $q$With 5 values sorted, the median is the middle (3rd) value: 8.$q$, $q${"9": "Computes the mean of the list instead of the median.", "10": "Picks the middle value by position miscount, landing on the 4th value instead of the 3rd.", "8.5": "Misapplies the even-count 'average the two middle values' rule to this odd-count (5-value) list, averaging 8 and 10."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-010$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 3, $q$The function f is defined by f(x) = x² − 4x + 3. For what value of x does f reach its minimum?$q$, $q$["2", "−2", "4", "3"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$The vertex x-coordinate is −b/(2a) = −(−4)/(2·1) = 2. The parabola opens upward, so the minimum occurs at x = 2.$q$, $q${"−2": "Drops the sign when computing −b/(2a), using −(4)/(2) instead of −(−4)/(2).", "4": "Reports the coefficient of the x term itself (from −4x) instead of computing the full vertex formula.", "3": "Confuses the minimum's x-coordinate with the constant term in the function."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-011$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 3, $q$A car rental costs $40 per day plus $0.20 per mile. If the total charge for a one-day rental was $70, how many miles were driven?$q$, $q$["150", "175", "125", "350"]$q$::jsonb, $q$150$q$, $q$70 = 40 + 0.20m → 30 = 0.20m → m = 150 miles.$q$, $q${"175": "Divides the full $70 charge by $0.20 without first subtracting the flat $40 fee (uses 35/0.2 halved incorrectly).", "125": "Subtracts the flat fee incorrectly (uses $40 minus a miscalculated rate) before dividing by $0.20.", "350": "Divides the entire $70 by $0.20 without subtracting the $40 flat fee at all."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-012$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 3, $q$A circle has a circumference of 12π. What is its area?$q$, $q$["36π", "12π", "6π", "144π"]$q$::jsonb, $q$36π$q$, $q$Circumference = 2πr = 12π → r = 6. Area = πr² = π(6²) = 36π.$q$, $q${"12π": "Reports the circumference itself instead of computing the area.", "6π": "Uses the radius (6) directly as if it were the area, without squaring it and multiplying by π.", "144π": "Uses the diameter (12) as the radius when squaring, computing π(12²) instead of π(6²)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-009$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 1, $q$Urban beekeeping has surged in popularity over the past decade, with hobbyists installing hives on rooftops from Brooklyn to Berlin. Advocates argue that city bees, insulated from the pesticides common in industrial agriculture, often produce healthier colonies than their rural counterparts, and that the hobby quietly bolsters local plant pollination in parks and gardens.

Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Urban beekeeping has grown popular and may offer ecological benefits to cities.", "Rural beekeeping is less safe than urban beekeeping.", "City parks depend entirely on hobbyist bees for pollination.", "Pesticides have made rural farming impossible."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Urban beekeeping has grown popular and may offer ecological benefits to cities.$q$, $q$The text describes the rise of urban beekeeping and two proposed benefits (healthier colonies, better pollination). The other choices overstate or distort details in the passage.$q$, $q${"Rural beekeeping is less safe than urban beekeeping.": "Turns a comparative detail (city bees insulated from pesticides) into a safety claim the passage never makes.", "City parks depend entirely on hobbyist bees for pollination.": "Inflates 'bolsters local plant pollination' into total dependence, an absolute claim the text doesn't support.", "Pesticides have made rural farming impossible.": "Wildly overstates the passage's brief mention of pesticides in industrial agriculture into a sweeping claim about farming."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-010$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$After decades of writing tightly plotted mysteries, novelist Elena Voss surprised critics with a sprawling, plotless meditation on memory. Some longtime fans felt betrayed by the departure; others praised it as the boldest work of her career.

Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To describe a shift in an author's writing and the mixed reaction it provoked", "To argue that mystery novels are inferior to literary fiction", "To criticize fans who disliked the new novel", "To summarize the plot of Voss's new book"]$q$::jsonb, $q$To describe a shift in an author's writing and the mixed reaction it provoked$q$, $q$The text describes Voss's stylistic departure and notes both negative and positive reactions to it, matching choice A. It takes no side, so B and C are unsupported, and it never summarizes a plot.$q$, $q${"To argue that mystery novels are inferior to literary fiction": "Mistakes the passage's neutral description of a genre shift for a value judgment it never makes.", "To criticize fans who disliked the new novel": "The passage reports mixed reactions without taking a side or criticizing anyone.", "To summarize the plot of Voss's new book": "Confuses the passage's focus (a stylistic shift and its reception) with plot summary, which never appears."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-011$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$Marine biologists have long suspected that some coral species can adapt to warmer waters over successive generations.

Which finding, if true, would most directly support the marine biologists' suspicion?$q$, $q$["Coral transplanted from a warm lagoon to a cooler reef showed significantly higher heat tolerance than coral native to the cooler reef.", "Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor.", "Warmer ocean temperatures are linked to more frequent storms.", "Many tourists visit coral reefs each year."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Coral transplanted from a warm lagoon to a cooler reef showed significantly higher heat tolerance than coral native to the cooler reef.$q$, $q$The claim is about coral adapting to heat across generations. Choice A directly ties prior exposure to warmth with greater heat tolerance, supporting the adaptation hypothesis.$q$, $q${"Coral reefs cover less than 1% of the ocean floor.": "A true-sounding fact about reef area that has no bearing on heat-tolerance or adaptation.", "Warmer ocean temperatures are linked to more frequent storms.": "Introduces an unrelated consequence of warming (storms) instead of evidence about coral adaptation.", "Many tourists visit coral reefs each year.": "Irrelevant popularity detail with no connection to the adaptation hypothesis."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-012$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A city planner argues that adding protected bike lanes reduces the number of accidents involving cyclists.

Which finding, if true, would most directly support the city planner's claim?$q$, $q$["After protected lanes were installed on Elm Street, cyclist injuries dropped 40% over two years despite a rise in the number of cyclists.", "Elm Street is two miles long.", "Cyclists prefer routes with fewer stoplights.", "The city spent $2 million on the lanes."]$q$::jsonb, $q$After protected lanes were installed on Elm Street, cyclist injuries dropped 40% over two years despite a rise in the number of cyclists.$q$, $q$The claim concerns accident reduction. Only choice A links the bike lanes to fewer injuries, and the drop is notable precisely because it occurred even as ridership increased.$q$, $q${"Elm Street is two miles long.": "A neutral fact about the street's length, unrelated to accident rates.", "Cyclists prefer routes with fewer stoplights.": "A plausible-sounding cyclist preference that says nothing about whether lanes reduce accidents.", "The city spent $2 million on the lanes.": "Cost information doesn't address the claim's actual subject: whether accidents decreased."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-013$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A researcher claims that students who take more practice tests score higher on final exams. In a study of 200 students, those who completed five or more practice tests averaged 88 on the final exam, while those who completed fewer than five averaged 74.

Which choice best describes data from the study that supports the researcher's claim?$q$, $q$["Students completing five or more practice tests averaged 14 points higher than those who completed fewer.", "All 200 students improved their scores over the semester.", "The study included students from ten different schools.", "Each practice test took about 30 minutes to complete."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Students completing five or more practice tests averaged 14 points higher than those who completed fewer.$q$, $q$The claim links practice-test frequency to higher scores. Choice A directly reports the 14-point gap between the two groups, which is the evidence supporting the claim.$q$, $q${"All 200 students improved their scores over the semester.": "Describes overall improvement without isolating the comparison between the two practice-test groups the claim is about.", "The study included students from ten different schools.": "A sample-diversity detail that doesn't report any score difference.", "Each practice test took about 30 minutes to complete.": "Logistical detail about test length, irrelevant to the score comparison."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-014$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 2, $q$A nutritionist claims a new diet leads to greater weight loss than a standard low-fat diet. Over 12 weeks, participants on the new diet lost an average of 9 pounds, compared with 5 pounds for those on the standard low-fat diet.

Which choice most directly supports the nutritionist's claim?$q$, $q$["Participants on the new diet lost an average of 4 more pounds than those on the standard diet.", "The study lasted 12 weeks.", "Some participants dropped out of the study before it ended.", "The new diet restricts carbohydrate intake."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Participants on the new diet lost an average of 4 more pounds than those on the standard diet.$q$, $q$The claim is comparative: the new diet caused greater weight loss. Only choice A states the actual difference in pounds lost between the two groups.$q$, $q${"The study lasted 12 weeks.": "Restates the study's duration instead of reporting the actual weight-loss difference.", "Some participants dropped out of the study before it ended.": "A methodological detail that doesn't quantify or support the comparative weight-loss claim.", "The new diet restricts carbohydrate intake.": "Describes what the diet involves rather than reporting its measured effect."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-015$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The museum's newest exhibit displays tools recovered from a 3,000-year-old settlement, including finely carved fishhooks and woven nets far more intricate than researchers expected from a community previously believed to rely solely on hunting.

Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The settlement's economy likely depended on fishing to a greater extent than previously assumed.", "The settlement had no contact with hunting communities.", "The fishhooks discovered were purely decorative and never used.", "Researchers had always known the community fished extensively."]$q$::jsonb, $q$The settlement's economy likely depended on fishing to a greater extent than previously assumed.$q$, $q$The discovery of intricate fishing tools 'far more' developed than expected, in a community 'believed to rely solely on hunting,' implies fishing played a bigger economic role than previously thought.$q$, $q${"The settlement had no contact with hunting communities.": "An unsupported absolute claim the passage never addresses.", "The fishhooks discovered were purely decorative and never used.": "Directly contradicts the point that the tools show fishing sophistication; the passage implies functional use.", "Researchers had always known the community fished extensively.": "Reverses the passage's actual point, which is that this find was more advanced than expected — i.e., a surprise, not prior knowledge."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-016$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$A software company's internal survey found that employees who worked remotely at least three days a week reported higher job satisfaction, yet the company's voluntary turnover rate stayed roughly the same after expanding remote options.

Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Job satisfaction and turnover rate are not necessarily driven by the same factors at this company.", "Remote work directly caused the turnover rate to increase.", "Every employee at the company prefers remote work.", "The company plans to eliminate remote work options."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Job satisfaction and turnover rate are not necessarily driven by the same factors at this company.$q$, $q$Satisfaction rose but turnover held steady, suggesting the two outcomes are not tightly linked at this company — exactly what choice A concludes without overreaching.$q$, $q${"Remote work directly caused the turnover rate to increase.": "Contradicts the passage, which says turnover stayed roughly the same, not that it increased.", "Every employee at the company prefers remote work.": "Overgeneralizes from a subset (those working remotely 3+ days) to all employees.", "The company plans to eliminate remote work options.": "Introduces a future action the passage never mentions or implies."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-017$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Obsidian, a volcanic glass prized by ancient toolmakers for its razor-sharp edges, can be chemically 'fingerprinted' to its volcanic source. By matching obsidian tools found at distant archaeological sites to specific quarries, researchers have traced trade routes spanning hundreds of miles, revealing exchange networks far more extensive than early excavators imagined.

Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Chemical analysis of obsidian tools has revealed unexpectedly far-reaching ancient trade networks.", "Obsidian is the sharpest natural material known to researchers.", "Early excavators correctly predicted the extent of ancient trade.", "Volcanic glass can only be found near active quarries."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Chemical analysis of obsidian tools has revealed unexpectedly far-reaching ancient trade networks.$q$, $q$The passage's point is that sourcing obsidian chemically has shown trade networks were more extensive than once believed — the opposite of choice C, and beyond the narrower details in B and D.$q$, $q${"Obsidian is the sharpest natural material known to researchers.": "Overextends the passage's mention of 'razor-sharp edges' into an unsupported superlative claim.", "Early excavators correctly predicted the extent of ancient trade.": "Reverses the passage's point that the networks were 'far more extensive than early excavators imagined.'", "Volcanic glass can only be found near active quarries.": "Misreads chemical 'fingerprinting' to a source quarry as a claim about where obsidian can be found at all."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-018$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 3, $q$A historian argues that a 19th-century famine was worsened by government policy rather than by crop failure alone.

Which choice would most directly support the historian's argument?$q$, $q$["Grain exports from the affected region continued at high volumes even as local food prices soared.", "The crop failure was caused by a fungal blight.", "The famine lasted for several consecutive years.", "Neighboring regions also experienced poor harvests that year."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Grain exports from the affected region continued at high volumes even as local food prices soared.$q$, $q$Continuing grain exports during a famine points to a policy choice worsening food scarcity, directly supporting the claim that policy — not just crop failure — deepened the crisis.$q$, $q${"The crop failure was caused by a fungal blight.": "Supports crop failure as a cause, which is the opposite of what the historian is arguing was the primary factor.", "The famine lasted for several consecutive years.": "A duration detail that doesn't address whether policy worsened the famine.", "Neighboring regions also experienced poor harvests that year.": "Suggests a shared natural cause across regions, which undercuts rather than supports a policy-specific explanation."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-019$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$Despite operating in one of the coldest inhabited regions on Earth, the research station relies almost entirely on solar power for six months of the year, switching to wind and stored batteries only during the darkest winter months.

Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["The region experiences significant seasonal variation in daylight.", "Solar power does not function in cold climates.", "Wind power is the station's primary energy source year-round.", "The station has no access to batteries during summer."]$q$::jsonb, $q$The region experiences significant seasonal variation in daylight.$q$, $q$The shift from solar to wind/batteries specifically in the 'darkest winter months' implies a seasonal light cycle extreme enough to make solar power impractical part of the year.$q$, $q${"Solar power does not function in cold climates.": "Contradicts the passage, which says the station relies on solar for six months despite the cold.", "Wind power is the station's primary energy source year-round.": "Reverses the roles described — wind/batteries are the backup used only in darkest winter, not the year-round primary source.", "The station has no access to batteries during summer.": "An unsupported absolute claim; the passage never says batteries are unavailable in summer, only that they're used mainly in winter."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-020$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 3, $q$For nearly a century, the landscapes of painter Clara Whitfield hung largely unseen in private collections, dismissed by early critics as derivative. A recent retrospective, drawing on newly uncovered letters, recasts her muted palettes and unconventional framing as deliberate innovations rather than technical shortcomings.

Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["A reevaluation of Whitfield's work suggests her once-dismissed techniques were intentional artistic choices.", "Whitfield's paintings were destroyed in a fire and are now lost.", "Critics have always admired Whitfield's use of color and framing.", "Whitfield primarily painted portraits rather than landscapes."]$q$::jsonb, $q$A reevaluation of Whitfield's work suggests her once-dismissed techniques were intentional artistic choices.$q$, $q$The retrospective reframes techniques once seen as flaws as deliberate choices — the reversal choice A captures. Choice C contradicts the passage's mention of early dismissal.$q$, $q${"Whitfield's paintings were destroyed in a fire and are now lost.": "Invents an event never mentioned in the passage, which instead says the works hung 'largely unseen' in collections — not destroyed.", "Critics have always admired Whitfield's use of color and framing.": "Directly contradicts the passage's statement that early critics dismissed her work as derivative.", "Whitfield primarily painted portraits rather than landscapes.": "Contradicts the passage's explicit description of her work as 'landscapes.'"}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-021$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Quantitative)$q$, 3, $q$An economist argues that a minimum-wage increase in City X did not significantly reduce employment. In the two years following the increase, City X's unemployment rate changed from 4.2% to 4.3%, while neighboring cities without the increase saw rates rise from 4.0% to 4.6%.

Which finding from the data most directly supports the economist's argument?$q$, $q$["City X's unemployment rate rose less than that of neighboring cities without the wage increase.", "City X's unemployment rate increased at all during the period.", "Neighboring cities are larger in population than City X.", "The wage increase in City X took effect immediately upon passage."]$q$::jsonb, $q$City X's unemployment rate rose less than that of neighboring cities without the wage increase.$q$, $q$The relevant comparison is City X's small rise (0.1 point) against a larger rise elsewhere (0.6 points) without the policy — implying the wage increase did not drive up unemployment relative to the trend.$q$, $q${"City X's unemployment rate increased at all during the period.": "Focuses on the fact of a small increase without the necessary comparison to the larger increase elsewhere that makes the finding meaningful.", "Neighboring cities are larger in population than City X.": "A population-size detail irrelevant to the unemployment-rate comparison.", "The wage increase in City X took effect immediately upon passage.": "A timing detail about implementation that says nothing about the employment outcome."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-022$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 2, $q$The novel's narrator repeatedly insists that she remembers every detail of that summer with perfect clarity, yet her account of the events shifts each time she retells it to a different character.

