  ($q$RW-028$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Inferences$q$, 3, $q$The city's new zoning ordinance permits accessory dwelling units on any residential lot, yet in its first year only a handful of homeowners applied for permits, most of them in the same three affluent neighborhoods.

Which choice is the most logical inference based on the text?$q$, $q$["Factors beyond legal permission, such as cost, may be limiting broader adoption of accessory dwelling units.", "No homeowners in the city are interested in accessory dwelling units.", "The zoning ordinance was overturned within its first year.", "Homeowners in affluent neighborhoods are barred from applying for permits."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Factors beyond legal permission, such as cost, may be limiting broader adoption of accessory dwelling units.$q$, $q$Legal permission alone didn't produce widespread uptake, and adoption clustered in wealthier areas — suggesting a nonlegal barrier, such as construction cost, is limiting broader participation.$q$, $q${"No homeowners in the city are interested in accessory dwelling units.": "Overstates low uptake as zero interest, contradicted by the 'handful' who did apply.", "The zoning ordinance was overturned within its first year.": "Invents an outcome never mentioned; the passage says the ordinance permits units, not that it was reversed.", "Homeowners in affluent neighborhoods are barred from applying for permits.": "Contradicts the passage, which says applicants were concentrated in affluent neighborhoods, not excluded from them."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-029$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Central Ideas and Details$q$, 2, $q$Translators of ancient epic poetry face a persistent dilemma: hew closely to the original's literal wording and risk producing stilted, unreadable verse, or prioritize the spirit and rhythm of the piece and risk drifting from its precise meaning. Recent translators have increasingly opted for the latter, arguing that a poem unread is a poem lost regardless of its fidelity.

Which choice best states the main idea of the text?$q$, $q$["Many modern translators favor readability and rhythm over strict literal accuracy when rendering ancient poetry.", "All translators agree that literal accuracy matters most.", "Ancient epic poetry cannot be translated into modern languages.", "Rhythm is irrelevant to the translation of poetry."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Many modern translators favor readability and rhythm over strict literal accuracy when rendering ancient poetry.$q$, $q$The passage states recent translators 'increasingly opted for' prioritizing spirit and rhythm over literal fidelity — the trend described in choice A.$q$, $q${"All translators agree that literal accuracy matters most.": "Contradicts the passage's description of a dilemma and a recent trend away from strict literal accuracy.", "Ancient epic poetry cannot be translated into modern languages.": "Contradicts the passage's entire premise, which discusses ongoing translation practice.", "Rhythm is irrelevant to the translation of poetry.": "Reverses the passage's point that rhythm is one of the qualities recent translators now prioritize."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-030$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Information and Ideas$q$, $q$Command of Evidence (Textual)$q$, 2, $q$A biologist argues that a species of frog has recently expanded its range northward due to warming temperatures.

