  ($q$RW-041$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Rather than resolving the mystery, the novel's final chapter only deepens the ______ surrounding the narrator's true identity.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["ambiguity", "clarity", "resolution", "certainty"]$q$::jsonb, $q$ambiguity$q$, $q$A final chapter that fails to resolve a mystery and instead 'deepens' the confusion is increasing ambiguity, not clarity or certainty.$q$, $q${"clarity": "Directly contradicts 'deepens,' which signals the confusion is increasing, not resolving into clarity.", "resolution": "Opposite of what happens — the chapter fails to resolve the mystery, per the sentence's first clause.", "certainty": "Contradicts a chapter that deepens uncertainty about identity rather than confirming it."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-042$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The city's new noise ordinance proved remarkably ______, cutting complaints in half within a single month of enforcement.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["effective", "controversial", "expensive", "confusing"]$q$::jsonb, $q$effective$q$, $q$Cutting complaints in half quickly is a mark of success, so 'effective' fits; the other choices aren't supported by the given result.$q$, $q${"controversial": "Not supported — cutting complaints in half describes success, not public disagreement.", "expensive": "Introduces a cost dimension the sentence never mentions.", "confusing": "Contradicts a policy that produced a clear, measurable positive outcome (fewer complaints)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-043$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Though the two economists ______ on almost every policy question, they found unexpected common ground on the issue of infrastructure spending.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["disagreed", "collaborated", "agreed", "lectured"]$q$::jsonb, $q$disagreed$q$, $q$'Though...unexpected common ground' signals a contrast: the economists must generally disagree for finding agreement to be notable.$q$, $q${"collaborated": "Contradicts the contrast signaled by 'Though...unexpected common ground,' which requires general disagreement, not collaboration, beforehand.", "agreed": "Directly opposite — if they usually agreed, finding common ground wouldn't be described as 'unexpected.'", "lectured": "An unrelated action that doesn't fit the sentence's contrast structure about policy positions."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-044$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The sculpture's ______ form — all sharp angles and exposed steel — stood in deliberate contrast to the soft curves of the surrounding garden.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["angular", "organic", "fragile", "invisible"]$q$::jsonb, $q$angular$q$, $q$'Sharp angles and exposed steel' describes an angular form, which the sentence explicitly contrasts with the garden's 'soft curves.'$q$, $q${"organic": "Contradicts 'sharp angles and exposed steel,' which describes a rigid, geometric form, not an organic one.", "fragile": "Not supported by the description, which emphasizes angularity and material, not durability.", "invisible": "Contradicts a sculpture explicitly described in visual detail and contrasted with its surroundings."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-045$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Editors praised the memoir for its ______ honesty, noting the author's willingness to describe her own failures in unflinching detail.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["unflinching", "guarded", "selective", "comedic"]$q$::jsonb, $q$unflinching$q$, $q$The sentence itself defines the quality: honesty shown by describing failures 'in unflinching detail,' so 'unflinching' matches directly.$q$, $q${"guarded": "Opposite of the honesty being praised — 'guarded' implies holding back, not describing failures in detail.", "selective": "Contradicts 'unflinching detail,' which implies thoroughness rather than picking and choosing what to reveal.", "comedic": "Introduces a tone (humor) the sentence never establishes; the focus is on candor about failure."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-046$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 1, $q$The negotiations remained ______ for months, with neither side willing to make the first concession.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["stalled", "swift", "cordial", "public"]$q$::jsonb, $q$stalled$q$, $q$Neither side making a concession 'for months' describes negotiations that are stalled, not swift.$q$, $q${"swift": "Directly contradicts 'for months,' which describes a slow, stuck process, not a fast one.", "cordial": "Confuses tone (friendliness) with progress; the sentence is about lack of movement, not mood.", "public": "Introduces visibility as a trait, unrelated to the sentence's focus on stalled progress."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-047$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$Unlike her predecessor's cautious approach, the new CEO's strategy was decidedly ______, entering three new markets within her first year.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["aggressive", "timid", "secretive", "gradual"]$q$::jsonb, $q$aggressive$q$, $q$Entering three new markets in one year, in contrast to a 'cautious' predecessor, describes an aggressive strategy, not a timid or gradual one.$q$, $q${"timid": "Directly contradicts the described contrast with the predecessor's caution and the aggressive move into three new markets.", "secretive": "Unrelated dimension; the sentence is about market expansion pace, not confidentiality.", "gradual": "Contradicts entering three markets 'within her first year,' which signals a fast, not gradual, strategy."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-048$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Words in Context$q$, 2, $q$The ancient manuscript, water-damaged and missing several pages, offers only a ______ account of the kingdom's final years.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?$q$, $q$["fragmentary", "comprehensive", "vivid", "official"]$q$::jsonb, $q$fragmentary$q$, $q$A manuscript that is 'water-damaged and missing several pages' would provide an incomplete, or fragmentary, account — not a comprehensive one.$q$, $q${"comprehensive": "Directly contradicts a damaged, incomplete manuscript missing several pages.", "vivid": "Confuses vividness of detail with completeness of the account, which the damage undermines.", "official": "Introduces a formality/authority concept the sentence never raises."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-049$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Ants are often assumed to navigate purely by scent trails left by other ants. To test this, researchers blocked all scent trails on a foraging path and found that ants still returned to the nest efficiently, apparently relying on a form of visual memory of landmarks.

Which choice best describes the function of the second sentence in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It presents evidence that challenges the assumption stated in the first sentence.", "It restates the assumption from the first sentence in different words.", "It introduces an unrelated topic about ant behavior.", "It summarizes the passage's conclusion before any evidence is given."]$q$::jsonb, $q$It presents evidence that challenges the assumption stated in the first sentence.$q$, $q$The experiment described in the second sentence shows ants navigating without scent trails, directly complicating the assumption introduced in the first sentence.$q$, $q${"It restates the assumption from the first sentence in different words.": "Misses that the second sentence reports a contrary experimental result, not a rephrasing of the assumption.", "It introduces an unrelated topic about ant behavior.": "The second sentence is directly related — it tests the very assumption stated first, not a new topic.", "It summarizes the passage's conclusion before any evidence is given.": "Reverses the passage's structure — the second sentence presents evidence, and no conclusion has been stated yet."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-050$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$Many assume that the invention of the printing press instantly made books cheap and widely available. In fact, for its first several decades, printed books remained luxury items, often more expensive than hand-copied manuscripts made by efficient scriptoria.

Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To complicate a popular assumption about the printing press's immediate effects", "To argue that hand-copied manuscripts were always superior to printed books", "To describe the mechanical process of operating a printing press", "To praise the individual credited with inventing the printing press"]$q$::jsonb, $q$To complicate a popular assumption about the printing press's immediate effects$q$, $q$The text opens with a common assumption and then complicates it with a historical detail ('in fact...'), matching choice A.$q$, $q${"To argue that hand-copied manuscripts were always superior to printed books": "Overreaches from one comparative fact (early printed books were pricier) into a sweeping superiority claim never made.", "To describe the mechanical process of operating a printing press": "The text discusses economic impact, not the mechanics of the press itself.", "To praise the individual credited with inventing the printing press": "The text focuses on an economic misconception, not on praising an inventor who isn't even mentioned."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-051$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A recent essay opens by describing the smell of rain on dry pavement, then pivots to a discussion of the chemical compound petrichor, before closing with a reflection on why humans across cultures find the scent pleasant.

Which choice best describes the overall structure of the essay?$q$, $q$["It moves from a sensory description to a scientific explanation to a broader reflection.", "It presents two competing scientific theories and evaluates each in turn.", "It argues against a widely held belief using historical evidence.", "It compares two different chemical compounds found in nature."]$q$::jsonb, $q$It moves from a sensory description to a scientific explanation to a broader reflection.$q$, $q$The essay's stated progression — sensory description, then chemistry, then reflection — matches choice A exactly.$q$, $q${"It presents two competing scientific theories and evaluates each in turn.": "Misreads a single linear progression (sensory → science → reflection) as a comparison of two competing theories.", "It argues against a widely held belief using historical evidence.": "The essay isn't structured as an argument against a belief; it's a descriptive progression through a topic.", "It compares two different chemical compounds found in nature.": "The passage discusses one compound (petrichor), not a comparison between two."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-052$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$The report's introduction outlines the rising cost of shipping. The middle section presents three case studies of companies that redesigned their supply chains. The conclusion offers general recommendations based on those cases.

Which choice best describes the function of the middle section of the report?$q$, $q$["To provide concrete examples that support the recommendations offered later", "To contradict the claims made in the report's introduction", "To define technical terms related to shipping logistics", "To criticize the companies discussed as case studies"]$q$::jsonb, $q$To provide concrete examples that support the recommendations offered later$q$, $q$Case studies presented before the conclusion's recommendations function as supporting evidence for those recommendations, matching choice A.$q$, $q${"To contradict the claims made in the report's introduction": "Misreads supporting case studies as opposition; nothing suggests the middle section undercuts the introduction.", "To define technical terms related to shipping logistics": "Confuses providing case studies with providing a glossary — no definitions are described.", "To criticize the companies discussed as case studies": "Reverses the function — case studies used to support recommendations aren't framed as criticism."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-053$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The author begins by praising the architect's early work, then spends the remainder of the essay detailing the ways his later buildings abandoned the principles that made the early work distinctive.

Which choice best describes the main purpose of the text?$q$, $q$["To trace a shift in the architect's style and suggest it represented a departure from his strengths", "To argue that the architect's later work was universally superior to his early work", "To provide a technical manual for building design", "To defend the architect against all criticism of his career"]$q$::jsonb, $q$To trace a shift in the architect's style and suggest it represented a departure from his strengths$q$, $q$The essay praises the early work, then shows how later work 'abandoned' those strengths — a structure that traces decline, not defense or unqualified praise.$q$, $q${"To argue that the architect's later work was universally superior to his early work": "Reverses the passage's actual point, which traces a decline from the distinctive early work.", "To provide a technical manual for building design": "Confuses a critical/biographical essay with an instructional document; no design instructions are described.", "To defend the architect against all criticism of his career": "Contradicts an essay that itself criticizes the later work's departure from earlier strengths."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-054$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 2, $q$A textbook chapter opens with a dramatic account of a shipwreck, then uses the incident throughout the chapter to illustrate principles of ocean currents and buoyancy.

Which choice best describes the function of the opening account in the text as a whole?$q$, $q$["It provides a memorable, concrete example that anchors abstract scientific concepts discussed later.", "It proves that shipwrecks are a common occurrence in the ocean.", "It argues against the value of studying ocean currents.", "It states the chapter's main conclusion before any explanation is given."]$q$::jsonb, $q$It provides a memorable, concrete example that anchors abstract scientific concepts discussed later.$q$, $q$Using a vivid narrative to introduce and later illustrate technical concepts is a common structural device, matching choice A.$q$, $q${"It proves that shipwrecks are a common occurrence in the ocean.": "Mistakes a single illustrative anecdote for a claim about the frequency of shipwrecks in general.", "It argues against the value of studying ocean currents.": "Contradicts the passage's use of the story specifically to teach ocean-current concepts.", "It states the chapter's main conclusion before any explanation is given.": "Misreads an illustrative opening example as a conclusion, when it instead sets up material explained afterward."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-055$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Text Structure and Purpose$q$, 3, $q$The op-ed acknowledges the strongest argument against its position before spending three paragraphs systematically responding to each part of that argument.

Which choice best describes the rhetorical strategy used in the text?$q$, $q$["Addressing a counterargument directly in order to strengthen the author's own position", "Ignoring opposing views entirely to avoid weakening the argument", "Presenting only anecdotal evidence without any structured argument", "Avoiding any clear thesis so that readers can draw their own conclusions"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Addressing a counterargument directly in order to strengthen the author's own position$q$, $q$Explicitly acknowledging and then systematically rebutting the strongest counterargument is a concession-and-rebuttal strategy, described accurately by choice A.$q$, $q${"Ignoring opposing views entirely to avoid weakening the argument": "Directly contradicts a strategy built on acknowledging, not ignoring, the counterargument.", "Presenting only anecdotal evidence without any structured argument": "Contradicts a text described as 'systematically responding,' which implies structure, not just anecdote.", "Avoiding any clear thesis so that readers can draw their own conclusions": "Contradicts a text that takes and defends a clear position by rebutting the strongest opposing case."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-056$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1: Economist A argues that remote work permanently reduced downtown office demand, citing vacancy rates that remain elevated three years after 2020.

Text 2: Economist B argues that office demand is cyclical and will rebound as companies mandate return-to-office policies, citing recent upticks in leasing activity in several major cities.

Based on the texts, how would Economist B most likely respond to Economist A's claim?$q$, $q$["By arguing that the elevated vacancy rates reflect a temporary cycle rather than a permanent shift", "By agreeing that office demand has disappeared permanently", "By dismissing all vacancy rate data as irrelevant to the discussion", "By claiming remote work never affected office demand at all"]$q$::jsonb, $q$By arguing that the elevated vacancy rates reflect a temporary cycle rather than a permanent shift$q$, $q$Economist B's position is that demand is cyclical, not permanently reduced, so B would frame A's elevated vacancy rates as part of a temporary cycle rather than a lasting shift.$q$, $q${"By agreeing that office demand has disappeared permanently": "Contradicts Economist B's stated position that demand is cyclical and will rebound, not permanently gone.", "By dismissing all vacancy rate data as irrelevant to the discussion": "Economist B uses similar data (leasing activity) rather than rejecting this category of evidence altogether.", "By claiming remote work never affected office demand at all": "Overstates B's position; B argues demand is cyclical, not that remote work had zero effect."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-057$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1: Historian A contends that the fall of the trade city was primarily caused by a shift in maritime trade routes away from its harbor.

Text 2: Historian B contends that internal political corruption, documented in surviving tax records, was the primary cause of the city's decline, occurring even before trade routes shifted.

Which choice best describes a key difference between the two historians' views?$q$, $q$["Historian A emphasizes an external economic cause, while Historian B emphasizes an internal political cause.", "Both historians agree entirely on the cause of the city's decline.", "Historian B relies exclusively on maritime trade records.", "Historian A blames the decline on political corruption."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Historian A emphasizes an external economic cause, while Historian B emphasizes an internal political cause.$q$, $q$Historian A points to trade routes (an external, economic factor), while Historian B points to corruption (an internal, political factor) — the contrast in choice A.$q$, $q${"Both historians agree entirely on the cause of the city's decline.": "Directly contradicts the passage, which sets up two distinct, competing causal explanations.", "Historian B relies exclusively on maritime trade records.": "Reverses the texts — Historian B relies on tax records, while maritime trade routes are Historian A's focus.", "Historian A blames the decline on political corruption.": "Swaps the two historians' positions; corruption is Historian B's explanation, not Historian A's."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-058$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Craft and Structure$q$, $q$Cross-Text Connections$q$, 3, $q$Text 1: Nutrition researcher A found that participants on a high-protein breakfast reported feeling full longer than those on a high-carbohydrate breakfast.

Text 2: Nutrition researcher B found no significant difference in reported fullness between high-protein and high-carbohydrate breakfasts, though her study used a considerably smaller sample size.

Which choice best describes the relationship between the findings of the two researchers?$q$, $q$["Researcher B's results do not confirm Researcher A's findings, though the smaller sample size may limit that comparison.", "Researcher B's findings exactly replicate Researcher A's results.", "Both researchers studied dinner rather than breakfast.", "Researcher A found no difference between the two meal types."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Researcher B's results do not confirm Researcher A's findings, though the smaller sample size may limit that comparison.$q$, $q$Researcher B's null result differs from Researcher A's positive result, but the passage notes B's smaller sample size, a detail that qualifies rather than fully undermines the comparison.$q$, $q${"Researcher B's findings exactly replicate Researcher A's results.": "Contradicts the passage, which says B found no significant difference — the opposite of replicating A's positive finding.", "Both researchers studied dinner rather than breakfast.": "Contradicts the passage, which specifies breakfast in both studies.", "Researcher A found no difference between the two meal types.": "Swaps the two researchers' findings; the null result belongs to Researcher B, not A."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-059$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The bridge was closed for repairs for nearly a year. ______ commuters were forced to take a lengthy detour through downtown.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["As a result,", "However,", "For instance,", "Similarly,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$As a result,$q$, $q$The detour was a consequence of the bridge closure, so a cause-and-effect transition like 'As a result' is needed.$q$, $q${"However,": "Misreads the relationship as a contrast when the detour is actually a direct consequence of the closure.", "For instance,": "Treats the detour as one example among several, when it's the sole, direct result being described.", "Similarly,": "Implies a comparison to another similar situation, but no second case is being compared here."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-060$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The committee reviewed dozens of proposals. ______ only two met every funding requirement.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Ultimately,", "Additionally,", "Likewise,", "Meanwhile,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Ultimately,$q$, $q$The second sentence states the final outcome of the review process, which 'Ultimately' correctly signals.$q$, $q${"Additionally,": "Treats the second sentence as an extra, separate point rather than the outcome of the review process just described.", "Likewise,": "Implies a parallel, similar situation rather than the concluding result of the same process.", "Meanwhile,": "Suggests a simultaneous but separate event, rather than the final outcome of the review already underway."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-061$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Many assumed the small bookstore would close within a year of the chain store's arrival. ______ it has since expanded to two more locations.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Instead,", "Consequently,", "Similarly,", "In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Instead,$q$, $q$The bookstore expanding contradicts the expectation that it would close, so a contrast word like 'Instead' is needed.$q$, $q${"Consequently,": "Treats the bookstore's survival as a logical result of the chain's arrival, when it actually defies that expectation.", "Similarly,": "Implies agreement with the first sentence's expectation, when the second sentence contradicts it.", "In addition,": "Treats the outcome as an additional fact rather than a reversal of the stated assumption."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-062$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The lecture covered the causes of the recession in great detail. ______ it left many questions about the recovery unanswered.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,", "Therefore,", "For example,", "In other words,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$However,$q$, $q$The second clause presents a limitation of the lecture, contrasting with the thoroughness described first, so 'However' fits.$q$, $q${"Therefore,": "Treats the lecture's gap as a result of its thoroughness, when it's actually a contrasting shortcoming.", "For example,": "Treats the unanswered questions as a specific instance of the causes covered, rather than a separate limitation.", "In other words,": "Implies the second clause restates the first, but it introduces a new, contrasting point instead."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-063$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The new policy reduced paperwork for small businesses. ______ it introduced a separate reporting requirement that many owners found equally burdensome.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["At the same time,", "As a result,", "For instance,", "Thus,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$At the same time,$q$, $q$The second sentence describes a simultaneous downside alongside the benefit mentioned first, which 'At the same time' captures.$q$, $q${"As a result,": "Treats the new requirement as caused by the reduced paperwork, when it's actually a separate, coexisting downside.", "For instance,": "Treats the new reporting requirement as an example of reduced paperwork, when it's the opposite — an added burden.", "Thus,": "Implies the second clause is a logical conclusion from the first, but it's a contrasting simultaneous effect instead."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-064$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$Researchers first hypothesized that the fish avoided the reef due to pollution. ______ water samples showed pollution levels well within normal range.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["However,", "Similarly,", "Specifically,", "Consequently,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$However,$q$, $q$The water sample results undercut the original hypothesis, so a contrast transition, 'However,' is required.$q$, $q${"Similarly,": "Implies the water samples confirm the hypothesis, when they actually contradict it.", "Specifically,": "Implies the second sentence narrows down or clarifies the hypothesis, when it actually disproves it.", "Consequently,": "Treats the normal pollution levels as a result of the hypothesis, rather than evidence against it."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-065$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The festival relies entirely on volunteer labor. ______ it manages to attract over 10,000 attendees each year.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,", "Consequently,", "For example,", "That is,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Nevertheless,$q$, $q$Attracting a large crowd despite relying only on volunteers is a contrast, which 'Nevertheless' signals.$q$, $q${"Consequently,": "Treats the festival's success as a result of relying on volunteers, when the sentence frames it as surprising despite that reliance.", "For example,": "Treats attendance as an instance of volunteer labor, rather than a contrasting outcome.", "That is,": "Implies restatement, but the second clause adds a contrasting fact rather than rephrasing the first."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-066$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The novel's first half follows a single narrator in painstaking detail. ______ the second half jumps between five different perspectives across two decades.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["By contrast,", "Similarly,", "As a result,", "In particular,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$By contrast,$q$, $q$A single narrator versus five shifting perspectives describes two structurally opposite halves, so 'By contrast' is correct.$q$, $q${"Similarly,": "Contradicts the sentence's description of two structurally opposite narrative approaches.", "As a result,": "Treats the shift in perspective as caused by the first half's structure, rather than as a contrasting structural choice.", "In particular,": "Implies the second half is a specific instance of the first half's approach, when it's actually its opposite."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-067$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The city increased funding for public transit by 20%. ______ ridership grew by only 3% over the following year.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nonetheless,", "Accordingly,", "Specifically,", "In addition,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Nonetheless,$q$, $q$A large funding increase producing only a small ridership gain is an unexpected, contrasting result, so 'Nonetheless' fits.$q$, $q${"Accordingly,": "Treats the small ridership gain as an expected result of the funding increase, when the sentence frames it as an underwhelming, contrasting outcome.", "Specifically,": "Implies the second sentence clarifies details of the funding increase, rather than presenting a separate, surprising result.", "In addition,": "Treats the ridership figure as an additional fact rather than a letdown relative to the funding increase."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-068$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 3, $q$The two researchers disagreed on nearly every methodological point. ______ they arrived at strikingly similar conclusions.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Yet,", "Because of this,", "Specifically,", "In short,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Yet,$q$, $q$Disagreeing on methodology but reaching similar conclusions is a contrast, best signaled by 'Yet.'$q$, $q${"Because of this,": "Treats the shared conclusions as caused by the methodological disagreement, when the relationship is actually one of contrast/surprise.", "Specifically,": "Implies the second clause narrows down the disagreement, but it introduces a contrasting outcome instead.", "In short,": "Implies a summary of what preceded, but the second clause adds a new, contrasting fact instead."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-069$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 2, $q$The company's earnings fell short of projections for the third straight quarter. ______ its stock price rose after the announcement, surprising analysts.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Surprisingly,", "Predictably,", "Similarly,", "As expected,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Surprisingly,$q$, $q$The passage itself notes the outcome 'surprised analysts,' so the transition should flag the unexpected result rather than call it predictable.$q$, $q${"Predictably,": "Contradicts the passage's own statement that the stock rise 'surprised analysts.'", "Similarly,": "Implies the stock movement matches the earnings trend, when it actually moves in the opposite, unexpected direction.", "As expected,": "Directly contradicts the explicit mention that the outcome surprised analysts."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-070$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Transitions$q$, 1, $q$The trail is only two miles long. ______ its steep elevation gain makes it one of the park's most difficult hikes.

Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?$q$, $q$["Nevertheless,", "Consequently,", "For instance,", "Likewise,"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Nevertheless,$q$, $q$A short trail that is still very difficult is a contrast in expectations, which 'Nevertheless' signals correctly.$q$, $q${"Consequently,": "Treats the trail's difficulty as a result of its short length, when short length would normally suggest an easy hike — the actual relationship is a contrast.", "For instance,": "Treats the elevation gain as an example of the trail's short length, rather than a contrasting factor that makes it hard despite being short.", "Likewise,": "Implies the difficulty aligns with expectations set by the trail's length, when it actually defies them."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-071$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Wright brothers made their first powered flight in 1903 near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.
• The flight lasted 12 seconds and covered 120 feet.
• Their success followed years of glider experiments.

The student wants to emphasize the brevity of the historic flight relative to its later significance. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Though it lasted only 12 seconds, the Wright brothers' 1903 flight near Kitty Hawk marked the dawn of powered aviation.", "The Wright brothers experimented with gliders for several years before 1903.", "Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, is known for its consistent winds.", "The Wright brothers' flight covered a distance of 120 feet."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Though it lasted only 12 seconds, the Wright brothers' 1903 flight near Kitty Hawk marked the dawn of powered aviation.$q$, $q$Choice A combines the flight's short duration (12 seconds) with its outsized historical significance, directly fulfilling the stated goal.$q$, $q${"The Wright brothers experimented with gliders for several years before 1903.": "Provides background on preparation but omits both the flight's brevity and its later significance, missing the stated goal.", "Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, is known for its consistent winds.": "An irrelevant geographic detail that addresses neither the flight's duration nor its significance.", "The Wright brothers' flight covered a distance of 120 feet.": "Mentions a numeric detail but distance, not duration, so it doesn't emphasize the flight's brevity as instructed."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-072$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.
• It has no bridges crossing its main stem.
• Much of its basin is inaccessible by road.

The student wants to emphasize the river's exceptional water volume. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.", "The Amazon River has no bridges crossing its main stem.", "Much of the Amazon basin cannot be reached by road.", "The Amazon flows through several South American countries."]$q$::jsonb, $q$The Amazon River discharges more water than the next seven largest rivers combined.$q$, $q$Only choice A directly conveys the river's water volume, which is the specific goal stated in the prompt.$q$, $q${"The Amazon River has no bridges crossing its main stem.": "An interesting fact about the river, but unrelated to water volume, the stated goal.", "Much of the Amazon basin cannot be reached by road.": "Describes accessibility, not the river's discharge volume.", "The Amazon flows through several South American countries.": "A geographic fact about the river's path, not its water volume."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-073$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Marie Curie won Nobel Prizes in both Physics (1903) and Chemistry (1911).
• She is the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences.
• She discovered the elements polonium and radium.

The student wants to highlight the uniqueness of Curie's achievement among Nobel laureates. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Marie Curie remains the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields.", "Marie Curie discovered the elements polonium and radium.", "Marie Curie won her first Nobel Prize in 1903.", "Nobel Prizes are awarded in several different categories."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Marie Curie remains the only person to have won Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields.$q$, $q$Choice A states the specific fact about Curie's singular distinction among laureates, matching the stated goal exactly.$q$, $q${"Marie Curie discovered the elements polonium and radium.": "A true and notable fact, but it doesn't address the uniqueness of her Nobel Prize achievement, the stated goal.", "Marie Curie won her first Nobel Prize in 1903.": "Gives a date but not the comparative uniqueness among laureates that the goal requires.", "Nobel Prizes are awarded in several different categories.": "General background on the prize system, not a statement about Curie's singular distinction."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-074$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Great Barrier Reef spans over 2,300 kilometers.
• It is visible from space.
• It comprises nearly 3,000 individual reefs.

The student wants to convey the reef's enormous scale using a single striking detail. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Great Barrier Reef is large enough to be visible from space.", "The Great Barrier Reef is home to a wide variety of marine life.", "Coral reefs face threats from rising ocean temperatures.", "The reef is made up of nearly 3,000 separate smaller reefs."]$q$::jsonb, $q$The Great Barrier Reef is large enough to be visible from space.$q$, $q$Being visible from space is the single most striking detail conveying scale, better fulfilling the goal than the more technical detail in choice D.$q$, $q${"The Great Barrier Reef is home to a wide variety of marine life.": "Describes biodiversity, not the single striking scale detail the goal calls for.", "Coral reefs face threats from rising ocean temperatures.": "An unrelated point about environmental threats, not about scale.", "The reef is made up of nearly 3,000 separate smaller reefs.": "A scale-related fact, but less striking/singular than being visible from space, which the goal calls for as 'a single striking detail.'"}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-075$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 1, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof.
• It was designed to be an easy-to-learn universal second language.
• Today it has an estimated 2 million speakers worldwide.

The student wants to introduce Esperanto by explaining the purpose behind its creation. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof as an easy-to-learn universal second language.", "Esperanto currently has an estimated 2 million speakers.", "L. L. Zamenhof lived during the 19th century.", "Many constructed languages have been invented over the centuries."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Esperanto was created in 1887 by L. L. Zamenhof as an easy-to-learn universal second language.$q$, $q$Choice A links the creator, date, and stated purpose (an easy-to-learn universal language), directly explaining why Esperanto was created.$q$, $q${"Esperanto currently has an estimated 2 million speakers.": "Describes present-day usage, not the purpose behind its creation, which is the stated goal.", "L. L. Zamenhof lived during the 19th century.": "A biographical detail about the creator's era, not the language's purpose.", "Many constructed languages have been invented over the centuries.": "General background on constructed languages, not Esperanto's specific creation purpose."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-076$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Octopuses have three hearts.
• Two pump blood to the gills; one pumps blood to the rest of the body.
• The main heart stops beating when the octopus swims.

The student wants to explain a surprising consequence of the octopus's unusual circulatory system. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Because its main heart stops beating while swimming, an octopus tires quickly and prefers to crawl.", "Octopuses have blood that appears blue due to a copper-based protein.", "Octopuses can be found in oceans throughout the world.", "Two of an octopus's three hearts pump blood to its gills."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Because its main heart stops beating while swimming, an octopus tires quickly and prefers to crawl.$q$, $q$Choice A connects a specific fact from the notes (the main heart stopping during swimming) to a consequence, fulfilling the goal of explaining a surprising effect.$q$, $q${"Octopuses have blood that appears blue due to a copper-based protein.": "An interesting circulatory fact, but not a consequence of the specific detail (the heart stopping while swimming) the goal asks about.", "Octopuses can be found in oceans throughout the world.": "A geographic fact unrelated to the circulatory system or its consequences.", "Two of an octopus's three hearts pump blood to its gills.": "States a structural fact about the hearts but not the surprising consequence the goal requires."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-077$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Library of Alexandria was one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.
• Its exact destruction date and cause remain debated by historians.
• It is believed to have held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.

The student wants to emphasize scholarly uncertainty about the library's demise. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Historians still debate exactly when and how the Library of Alexandria was destroyed.", "The Library of Alexandria held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.", "The library was among the largest in the ancient world.", "Alexandria was founded by Alexander the Great."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Historians still debate exactly when and how the Library of Alexandria was destroyed.$q$, $q$Only choice A directly addresses the ongoing scholarly debate about the library's destruction, matching the stated goal.$q$, $q${"The Library of Alexandria held hundreds of thousands of scrolls.": "Describes the library's size, not the uncertainty around its destruction, the stated goal.", "The library was among the largest in the ancient world.": "Another size-related fact that doesn't address scholarly uncertainty about its demise.", "Alexandria was founded by Alexander the Great.": "An unrelated historical fact about the city's founding, not the library's destruction."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-078$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Bamboo can grow up to 91 centimeters in a single day.
• It is technically classified as a grass, not a tree.
• Some species are used in construction due to their strength-to-weight ratio.

The student wants to highlight bamboo's remarkable growth rate. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Some bamboo species can grow as much as 91 centimeters in a single day.", "Bamboo is classified as a grass rather than a tree.", "Bamboo's strength-to-weight ratio makes it useful in construction.", "Bamboo grows in a variety of climates around the world."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Some bamboo species can grow as much as 91 centimeters in a single day.$q$, $q$Only choice A states a growth-rate statistic, which directly accomplishes the stated goal of highlighting bamboo's growth speed.$q$, $q${"Bamboo is classified as a grass rather than a tree.": "A true taxonomic fact, but it doesn't address growth rate, the stated goal.", "Bamboo's strength-to-weight ratio makes it useful in construction.": "Describes a structural property, not growth speed.", "Bamboo grows in a variety of climates around the world.": "Describes geographic range, not the rate of growth."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-079$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets in the solar system.
• A day on Venus is longer than its year.
• Its surface temperature is hot enough to melt lead.

The student wants to introduce a fact about Venus's unusual relationship between its day length and year length. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["A single day on Venus lasts longer than a full Venusian year.", "Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets.", "Venus's surface is hot enough to melt lead.", "Venus is the second planet from the sun."]$q$::jsonb, $q$A single day on Venus lasts longer than a full Venusian year.$q$, $q$Choice A is the only option that addresses the specific relationship between day length and year length named in the prompt.$q$, $q${"Venus rotates in the opposite direction from most planets.": "A true fact about Venus, but it addresses rotation direction, not the day-length-vs-year-length relationship the goal specifies.", "Venus's surface is hot enough to melt lead.": "A temperature fact unrelated to the day/year length comparison.", "Venus is the second planet from the sun.": "A basic positional fact that doesn't address the specific relationship the goal calls for."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-080$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 3, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Sahara Desert was once a lush, green savanna.
• This period, called the African Humid Period, ended roughly 5,000 years ago.
• Cave paintings in the region depict animals like giraffes and hippos.

The student wants to use a specific piece of evidence to support the claim that the Sahara was once green. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Cave paintings in the region depict animals such as giraffes and hippos, species associated with wetter climates.", "The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.", "The African Humid Period ended about 5,000 years ago.", "Deserts can form for a variety of different climatic reasons."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Cave paintings in the region depict animals such as giraffes and hippos, species associated with wetter climates.$q$, $q$Only choice A offers concrete supporting evidence (cave paintings of animals tied to wetter climates) for the claim, rather than simply restating it.$q$, $q${"The Sahara is the largest hot desert in the world.": "A scale fact about the desert today, not evidence that it was once green.", "The African Humid Period ended about 5,000 years ago.": "Provides timing but not concrete evidence (like the cave paintings) supporting the claim of a once-green Sahara.", "Deserts can form for a variety of different climatic reasons.": "General background on desert formation, not specific evidence for the Sahara's past state."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-081$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Honey never spoils if stored properly.
• Archaeologists have found 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that was still edible.
• Honey's low moisture content and acidity prevent bacterial growth.

The student wants to provide a concrete historical example that illustrates honey's exceptional shelf life. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Archaeologists have discovered 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that remained edible.", "Honey's low moisture content helps prevent bacterial growth.", "Honey is produced by bees from flower nectar.", "Egyptian tombs have been found to contain many preserved goods."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Archaeologists have discovered 3,000-year-old honey in Egyptian tombs that remained edible.$q$, $q$Choice A gives the specific historical example (ancient Egyptian honey) requested by the prompt, rather than the general explanation in choice B.$q$, $q${"Honey's low moisture content helps prevent bacterial growth.": "Explains the mechanism behind honey's shelf life but isn't the concrete historical example the goal asks for.", "Honey is produced by bees from flower nectar.": "General background on how honey is made, unrelated to shelf life or historical evidence.", "Egyptian tombs have been found to contain many preserved goods.": "Too general — doesn't cite the specific honey example needed to fulfill the goal."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-082$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• The Eiffel Tower grows about 15 centimeters taller in summer.
• This is due to thermal expansion of the iron.
• It was originally intended to be a temporary structure.

The student wants to explain why the Eiffel Tower's height changes seasonally. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["The Eiffel Tower's iron structure expands in warmer temperatures, making it about 15 centimeters taller in summer.", "The Eiffel Tower was originally meant to be a temporary structure.", "The Eiffel Tower is a famous landmark located in Paris.", "Iron is a metal that is commonly used in construction."]$q$::jsonb, $q$The Eiffel Tower's iron structure expands in warmer temperatures, making it about 15 centimeters taller in summer.$q$, $q$Only choice A connects the cause (thermal expansion) to the effect (a 15-centimeter height change), directly explaining the phenomenon.$q$, $q${"The Eiffel Tower was originally meant to be a temporary structure.": "A true historical fact, but unrelated to the cause of its seasonal height change.", "The Eiffel Tower is a famous landmark located in Paris.": "General identifying information, not an explanation of the height change.", "Iron is a metal that is commonly used in construction.": "General material fact that doesn't connect thermal expansion to the tower's specific height change."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-083$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Expression of Ideas$q$, $q$Rhetorical Synthesis$q$, 2, $q$A student has gathered the following notes:
• Sea otters hold hands while sleeping to keep from drifting apart.
• They wrap themselves in kelp for the same reason.
• Sea otters are a keystone species in kelp forest ecosystems.

The student wants to describe a specific behavior otters use to stay together while resting. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?$q$, $q$["Sea otters often hold hands while sleeping so they don't drift apart from one another.", "Sea otters are considered a keystone species in their ecosystem.", "Kelp forests support a wide diversity of marine life.", "Sea otters live primarily along the Pacific coast."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Sea otters often hold hands while sleeping so they don't drift apart from one another.$q$, $q$Choice A directly describes the specific resting behavior (holding hands) requested by the prompt.$q$, $q${"Sea otters are considered a keystone species in their ecosystem.": "An ecological fact about otters' broader role, not the specific resting behavior the goal asks about.", "Kelp forests support a wide diversity of marine life.": "Describes the ecosystem generally, not the otters' specific behavior for staying together.", "Sea otters live primarily along the Pacific coast.": "A geographic/range fact unrelated to the described resting behavior."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-084$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Once the votes were tallied______the results were announced to a stunned crowd.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ", "; ", ": ", " "]$q$::jsonb, $q$, $q$, $q$'Once the votes were tallied' is an introductory dependent clause and must be set off from the main clause with a comma.$q$, $q${"; ": "Wrongly treats the introductory dependent clause as if it could stand alone as an independent clause.", ": ": "Misapplies colon usage, which introduces an explanation or list, not an introductory dependent clause.", " ": "Fails to separate the introductory clause from the main clause at all, creating a run-on."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-085$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The city's water tower had stood for over a century______no one wanted to see it demolished.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ", ", ", " so, ", ": "]$q$::jsonb, $q$; $q$, $q$Both parts are independent clauses with no coordinating conjunction, so they must be joined with a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$, $q${", ": "Creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma and no conjunction.", " so, ": "Misplaces the comma before the conjunction 'so' rather than using it correctly, and doesn't fit the semicolon-based joining the sentence needs without a conjunction.", ": ": "Misapplies colon usage; there's no list or explanation being introduced, just two related independent clauses."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-086$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The experiment's outcome confirmed the hypothesis______heat does accelerate the reaction.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ", "; ", ", ", " "]$q$::jsonb, $q$: $q$, $q$A colon correctly introduces the explanation of what the hypothesis was, following a complete independent clause.$q$, $q${"; ": "Misapplies the semicolon, which joins two independent clauses of equal weight rather than introducing an explanation of the hypothesis.", ", ": "A comma alone can't properly introduce the explanatory clause that follows.", " ": "Fails to punctuate the boundary at all, running the explanation into the clause without warning the reader."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-087$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Employees______who complete the training will receive a certificate.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ", ", ", "; ", ": "]$q$::jsonb, $q$ $q$, $q$'Who complete the training' is a restrictive (essential) clause identifying which employees, so no comma is needed before it.$q$, $q${", ": "Incorrectly treats 'who complete the training' as a nonessential aside requiring commas, when it's actually essential to identifying which employees.", "; ": "Misapplies the semicolon, which joins independent clauses, not a restrictive clause to its noun.", ": ": "Misapplies the colon, which introduces explanations or lists, not a restrictive modifying clause."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-088$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The recipe, ______ was passed down through four generations, calls for a pinch of saffron.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which", "that", "who", "whom"]$q$::jsonb, $q$which$q$, $q$The clause is nonessential (set off by commas) and refers to a thing, the recipe, so 'which' is correct; 'that' is reserved for restrictive clauses.$q$, $q${"that": "Uses the relative pronoun reserved for restrictive (essential) clauses, but this clause is nonessential and set off by commas.", "who": "Uses a pronoun reserved for people, but the antecedent here is a thing (the recipe).", "whom": "Same error as 'who' — a pronoun for people, misapplied to a thing, and additionally the wrong case for a clause subject."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-089$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Ticket sales exceeded expectations______therefore, the band added a second show.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ", ", ", ": ", " "]$q$::jsonb, $q$; $q$, $q$A conjunctive adverb like 'therefore' joining two independent clauses requires a semicolon before it (and a comma after).$q$, $q${", ": "A comma alone can't join two independent clauses before a conjunctive adverb like 'therefore' — it creates a comma splice.", ": ": "Misapplies the colon; 'therefore' functions as a conjunctive adverb linking two independent clauses, which needs a semicolon, not a colon.", " ": "Fails to punctuate the clause boundary before 'therefore' at all, creating a run-on."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-090$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Exhausted from the overnight flight______the team went straight to bed instead of sightseeing.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ", "; ", ": ", " "]$q$::jsonb, $q$, $q$, $q$'Exhausted from the overnight flight' is an introductory participial phrase and must be followed by a comma.$q$, $q${"; ": "Wrongly treats the introductory participial phrase as if it were a complete independent clause needing a semicolon.", ": ": "Misapplies the colon, which isn't used to set off an introductory descriptive phrase.", " ": "Fails to separate the introductory phrase from the main clause, running them together."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-091$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The rain continued for hours______the streets began to flood.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ", ", ", ": ", " "]$q$::jsonb, $q$; $q$, $q$Two independent clauses with no conjunction must be separated by a semicolon; a comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$, $q${", ": "Creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma.", ": ": "Misapplies the colon; the second clause is a related independent statement, not an explanation or list introduced by the first.", " ": "Fails to separate the two independent clauses at all, creating a run-on."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-092$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The museum acquired three new pieces this year______a bronze sculpture, an oil painting, and a hand-woven tapestry.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": ", "; ", ", ", ""]$q$::jsonb, $q$: $q$, $q$A colon correctly introduces the list of items following the complete independent clause.$q$, $q${"; ": "Misapplies the semicolon, which joins two independent clauses, not a clause and the list that explains it.", ", ": "A comma alone is too weak to introduce a list following a complete independent clause here.", "": "Fails to signal the upcoming list at all, running the sentence together without warning the reader."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-093$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$In the years following the merger______the company's revenue nearly tripled.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ", "; ", ": ", " "]$q$::jsonb, $q$, $q$, $q$'In the years following the merger' is an introductory phrase and requires a comma before the main clause.$q$, $q${"; ": "Wrongly treats the introductory prepositional phrase as an independent clause requiring a semicolon.", ": ": "Misapplies the colon to an introductory phrase rather than an explanation or list.", " ": "Fails to separate the introductory phrase from the main clause."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-094$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Critics were divided on the film's pacing______audiences overwhelmingly loved it.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ", ", ", ": ", " "]$q$::jsonb, $q$; $q$, $q$Two independent clauses with a contrast in meaning but no coordinating conjunction must be joined with a semicolon.$q$, $q${", ": "Creates a comma splice by joining two independent, contrasting clauses with only a comma.", ": ": "Misapplies the colon; the second clause offers a contrast, not an explanation introduced by the first.", " ": "Fails to punctuate the boundary between the two independent clauses, creating a run-on."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-095$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The award, ______ given annually since 1962, honors excellence in local journalism.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which has been", "that has been", "who has been", "being"]$q$::jsonb, $q$which has been$q$, $q$The nonessential clause set off by commas refers to a thing (the award) and needs a complete verb phrase, so 'which has been' is correct.$q$, $q${"that has been": "Uses 'that,' reserved for restrictive clauses, on a nonessential clause set off by commas.", "who has been": "Uses a pronoun for people on an antecedent that is a thing (the award).", "being": "Drops the auxiliary 'has' needed for the present-perfect meaning ('given annually since 1962'), leaving an incomplete verb phrase."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-096$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The bakery sold out of croissants by nine a.m.______the line had formed before sunrise.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ", ", ", ": ", " because "]$q$::jsonb, $q$; $q$, $q$Both clauses are independent and closely related, so a semicolon correctly joins them without a coordinating conjunction.$q$, $q${", ": "Creates a comma splice between two closely related independent clauses.", ": ": "Misapplies the colon; the second clause explains a cause but isn't a list or a formal explanation introduced by a colon after this kind of independent clause.", " because ": "Adds a subordinating conjunction not licensed by the sentence's structure, and combined with no comma, doesn't fit the intended two-independent-clause relationship shown by the answer choices' pattern."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-097$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The flock of geese______south every autumn.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["migrates", "migrate", "have migrated", "are migrating"]$q$::jsonb, $q$migrates$q$, $q$'Flock' is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb 'migrates.'$q$, $q${"migrate": "Treats 'flock' as if it were plural, ignoring that collective nouns like 'flock' take singular verbs.", "have migrated": "Introduces present-perfect tense, which doesn't match the simple, habitual action described ('every autumn').", "are migrating": "Introduces present-progressive tense, describing an action in progress right now rather than a recurring, general truth."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-098$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the coach nor the players______satisfied with the referee's call.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were", "was", "is", "has been"]$q$::jsonb, $q$were$q$, $q$In an 'either/or' or 'neither/nor' construction, the verb agrees with the nearer subject, 'players,' which is plural, so 'were' is correct.$q$, $q${"was": "Wrongly agrees the verb with the singular 'coach' instead of the nearer subject 'players,' which governs agreement in neither/nor constructions.", "is": "Same nearer-subject error as 'was,' plus the wrong tense for a general/past context implied by the sentence.", "has been": "Uses a singular present-perfect form that doesn't match the plural 'players' governing agreement."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-099$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Each of the applicants______required to submit three letters of recommendation.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is", "are", "were", "have been"]$q$::jsonb, $q$is$q$, $q$'Each' is singular, regardless of the plural noun in the prepositional phrase that follows it, so the singular verb 'is' is correct.$q$, $q${"are": "Wrongly agrees the verb with the plural noun 'applicants' in the prepositional phrase instead of the singular subject 'Each.'", "were": "Same subject-verb agreement error as 'are,' plus the wrong (past) tense.", "have been": "Uses a plural present-perfect form, still disagreeing with the singular subject 'Each.'"}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the time the guests arrive, she______dinner for two hours.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will have been cooking", "cooks", "cooked", "is cooking"]$q$::jsonb, $q$will have been cooking$q$, $q$'By the time' with a future event signals the future perfect progressive tense, 'will have been cooking,' to show an action continuing up to that future point.$q$, $q${"cooks": "Uses simple present tense, which doesn't convey an action continuing up to a future point in time.", "cooked": "Uses simple past tense, which doesn't match the future time frame set by 'by the time the guests arrive.'", "is cooking": "Uses present progressive, describing an action happening now rather than one that will have been ongoing by a future moment."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The data______surprising, prompting the team to repeat the experiment.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were", "was", "is", "has been"]$q$::jsonb, $q$were$q$, $q$In formal Standard English, 'data' is treated as a plural noun (the plural of 'datum'), so it takes the plural verb 'were.'$q$, $q${"was": "Treats 'data' as a singular noun, ignoring its (formally correct) plural usage as the plural of 'datum.'", "is": "Same singular-agreement error as 'was,' plus the wrong tense for the past-context sentence.", "has been": "Uses a singular present-perfect form that still disagrees with 'data' treated as plural."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The company reminded its employees to submit______expense reports by Friday.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their", "his", "its", "one's"]$q$::jsonb, $q$their$q$, $q$The antecedent 'employees' is plural, so the plural possessive pronoun 'their' is required for agreement.$q$, $q${"his": "Uses a singular, gendered pronoun that doesn't agree with the plural antecedent 'employees.'", "its": "Uses a pronoun for a non-person/singular entity, mismatched with the plural, human antecedent 'employees.'", "one's": "Uses a singular indefinite pronoun, mismatched with the plural antecedent 'employees.'"}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The twins each brought______own laptop to the study session.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their", "his", "its", "one's"]$q$::jsonb, $q$their$q$, $q$'The twins' is the plural antecedent, so 'their' is the only pronoun among the choices that agrees with it correctly.$q$, $q${"his": "Uses a singular, gendered pronoun that doesn't agree with the plural antecedent 'twins.'", "its": "Uses a pronoun for a non-person/singular entity, mismatched with the plural, human antecedent 'twins.'", "one's": "Uses a singular indefinite pronoun, mismatched with the plural antecedent 'twins.'"}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Walking through the old district, ______

Which choice most logically and clearly completes the sentence?$q$, $q$["I finally saw the cathedral's spires come into view.", "the cathedral's spires finally came into view.", "the old district's history was learned by me.", "cathedral spires were seen by tourists walking through the district."]$q$::jsonb, $q$I finally saw the cathedral's spires come into view.$q$, $q$The introductory phrase 'Walking through the old district' describes a person, not the spires or the history, so the main clause's subject must be the walker: 'I.'$q$, $q${"the cathedral's spires finally came into view.": "Creates a dangling modifier — 'Walking through the old district' describes a person, but this choice makes the spires the ones doing the walking.", "the old district's history was learned by me.": "Creates the same dangling-modifier problem, since 'history' can't be the one walking, and additionally shifts to an awkward passive construction.", "cathedral spires were seen by tourists walking through the district.": "Shifts the walker to 'tourists,' introducing a new subject not established by the sentence and still mismatching the intended modifier logic."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The internship taught her to manage budgets, delegate tasks, and______client meetings.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lead", "leading", "how to lead", "she led"]$q$::jsonb, $q$lead$q$, $q$The sentence lists parallel infinitive actions ('to manage,' '[to] delegate,' '[to] lead'), so 'lead' maintains the parallel structure.$q$, $q${"leading": "Breaks the parallel structure of infinitive verbs ('to manage, delegate, and lead') by switching to a gerund form.", "how to lead": "Breaks parallelism by adding an extra phrase structure instead of matching the plain infinitive form used by the other list items.", "she led": "Breaks parallelism entirely by introducing a full subject-verb clause instead of matching the infinitive list."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The award recognizes work that is original, rigorous, and______.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["clearly written", "clarity in writing", "written with clarity", "the writing is clear"]$q$::jsonb, $q$clearly written$q$, $q$The list requires parallel adjective phrases ('original, rigorous, and ___'), and 'clearly written' matches that adjectival form.$q$, $q${"clarity in writing": "Breaks the parallel adjective structure ('original, rigorous, and ___') by using a noun phrase instead of an adjective phrase.", "written with clarity": "Breaks parallelism by using a different grammatical structure (past participle + prepositional phrase) than the adjectives it should match.", "the writing is clear": "Breaks parallelism entirely by introducing a full clause instead of an adjective matching 'original' and 'rigorous.'"}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Yesterday, the technician inspected the wiring and______that a fuse had blown.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["determined", "determines", "will determine", "determining"]$q$::jsonb, $q$determined$q$, $q$The sentence is in past tense ('Yesterday...inspected'), so the second verb must also be past tense, 'determined,' to remain consistent.$q$, $q${"determines": "Shifts to present tense, breaking consistency with the past-tense 'inspected' earlier in the sentence.", "will determine": "Shifts to future tense, which doesn't match the past-tense narrative established by 'Yesterday...inspected.'", "determining": "Drops the finite verb form needed to complete the second half of the compound predicate, leaving an incomplete construction."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$RW-108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Of the two finalists, the second speaker was the______prepared.

Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["more", "most", "much", "many"]$q$::jsonb, $q$more$q$, $q$When comparing exactly two things, the comparative form 'more' is required rather than the superlative 'most,' which is used for three or more.$q$, $q${"most": "Uses the superlative form, reserved for comparisons among three or more, when only two finalists are being compared.", "much": "Uses a non-comparative intensifier that doesn't correctly form a two-item comparison.", "many": "Uses a quantity word that doesn't fit as a comparative modifier for 'prepared' at all."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-013$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, $q$If 4x − 9 = 23, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["8", "6", "3.5", "32"]$q$::jsonb, $q$8$q$, $q$4x − 9 = 23 → 4x = 32 → x = 8.$q$, $q${"6": "Drops the −9 term entirely and divides 23 by 4 (≈5.75), rounding to 6.", "3.5": "Sign error — subtracts 9 instead of adding it when isolating 4x, computing (23 − 9)/4.", "32": "Correctly isolates 4x = 32 but stops there, forgetting to divide by 4."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-014$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, $q$If 2(x + 3) = 5x − 6, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4", "2", "6", "−4"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4$q$, $q$2x + 6 = 5x − 6 → 12 = 3x → x = 4.$q$, $q${"2": "Divides the correct 3x = 12 by an incorrect coefficient (double-counting the distributed 2), landing on x = 2.", "6": "Loses track of a sign while combining the constant terms, arriving at 3x = 18 instead of 3x = 12.", "−4": "Flips the signs of both constants when moving terms (2x − 6 = 5x + 6 instead of 2x + 6 = 5x − 6), producing the negative of the correct answer."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-015$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, $q$A line passes through the points (2, 5) and (4, 11). What is the y-intercept of the line?$q$, $q$["−1", "1", "5", "11"]$q$::jsonb, $q$−1$q$, $q$Slope = (11 − 5)/(4 − 2) = 3. Using y = 3x + b with (2, 5): 5 = 3(2) + b → b = −1.$q$, $q${"1": "Uses the correct slope but a sign error when solving for b, adding instead of subtracting.", "5": "Reports the y-coordinate of the first given point instead of solving for the actual y-intercept.", "11": "Reports the y-coordinate of the second given point instead of solving for the y-intercept."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-016$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, $q$If x + y = 10 and x − y = 2, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["4", "6", "8", "2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4$q$, $q$Subtract the equations: (x + y) − (x − y) = 10 − 2 → 2y = 8 → y = 4.$q$, $q${"6": "Solves for x (x = 6) using the standard elimination method but mistakenly reports it as the answer for y.", "8": "Correctly finds 2y = 8 by subtracting the equations but forgets to divide by 2.", "2": "Misreads the equation x − y = 2 as directly stating the value of y, skipping the needed algebra."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-017$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 3x − 5 > 10?$q$, $q$["6", "5", "4", "15"]$q$::jsonb, $q$6$q$, $q$3x − 5 > 10 → 3x > 15 → x > 5, so the smallest integer satisfying this is 6.$q$, $q${"5": "Treats the inequality as if it were 'greater than or equal to,' including the boundary value 5 itself.", "4": "Rounds in the wrong direction when finding the smallest integer greater than 5, landing one below the boundary.", "15": "Forgets to divide by 3 after reaching 3x > 15 and reports the intermediate value instead."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-018$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, $q$A gym membership costs $25 to join plus $15 per month. If a member has paid a total of $205, how many months has the membership been active?$q$, $q$["12", "10", "13", "180"]$q$::jsonb, $q$12$q$, $q$25 + 15m = 205 → 15m = 180 → m = 12.$q$, $q${"10": "Divides the total by the $20 join-plus-something confusion instead of correctly isolating and dividing by the $15 monthly rate.", "13": "Forgets to subtract the $25 joining fee before dividing, computing 205 ÷ 15 ≈ 13.67 and rounding down.", "180": "Correctly isolates 15m = 180 but stops there, forgetting the final division by 15."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-019$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, $q$If y = 2x − 1 and 3x + y = 14, what is the value of x + y?$q$, $q$["8", "3", "5", "11"]$q$::jsonb, $q$8$q$, $q$Substitute: 3x + (2x − 1) = 14 → 5x = 15 → x = 3, so y = 2(3) − 1 = 5. Thus x + y = 8.$q$, $q${"3": "Solves correctly for x (x = 3) but reports it alone instead of computing x + y.", "5": "Solves correctly for y (y = 5) but reports it alone instead of computing x + y.", "11": "Miscombines the substitution, computing 14 − x = 11 instead of correctly solving the system for x + y."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-020$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, $q$The function f is linear, with f(0) = 4 and f(2) = 10. What is the value of f(5)?$q$, $q$["19", "14", "10", "25"]$q$::jsonb, $q$19$q$, $q$Slope = (10 − 4)/(2 − 0) = 3, so f(x) = 3x + 4. Then f(5) = 3(5) + 4 = 19.$q$, $q${"14": "Miscalculates the slope as 2 instead of 3, computing 2(5) + 4 = 14.", "10": "Simply restates the given value f(2) = 10 instead of extending the function to x = 5.", "25": "Uses f(2) = 10 as the intercept instead of f(0) = 4, computing 3(5) + 10 = 25."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-021$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, $q$A line given by y = 2x + 3 is parallel to a second line that passes through (0, −1). What is the slope of the second line?$q$, $q$["2", "−1", "3", "1/2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$Parallel lines have equal slopes, so the second line also has slope 2.$q$, $q${"−1": "Mistakes the given point's y-coordinate (−1) for the slope itself.", "3": "Mistakes the y-intercept constant (3) from y = 2x + 3 for the slope value.", "1/2": "Uses the negative-reciprocal rule for perpendicular lines instead of the equal-slope rule for parallel lines."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-022$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, $q$If |x − 4| = 9, what is the sum of all possible values of x?$q$, $q$["8", "9", "−5", "13"]$q$::jsonb, $q$8$q$, $q$x − 4 = 9 gives x = 13; x − 4 = −9 gives x = −5. The sum of the two solutions is 13 + (−5) = 8.$q$, $q${"9": "Reports the absolute-value target (9) itself instead of solving for and summing the two x-values.", "−5": "Solves correctly for one solution (x = −5) but reports it alone instead of the sum of both solutions.", "13": "Solves correctly for the other solution (x = 13) but reports it alone instead of the sum of both solutions."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-023$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 3, $q$How many solutions does the system y = 2x + 5 and y = 2x + 3 have?$q$, $q$["0", "1", "2", "Infinitely many"]$q$::jsonb, $q$0$q$, $q$Setting the equations equal: 2x + 5 = 2x + 3 → 5 = 3, which is false. The lines are parallel with different intercepts, so there is no solution.$q$, $q${"1": "Assumes any two linear equations must intersect exactly once, without checking that the slopes are equal.", "2": "Assumes a system of two linear equations can have two solutions, a case that isn't possible for two lines.", "Infinitely many": "Confuses this system (same slope, different intercepts) with the case of identical lines, which is what actually produces infinitely many solutions."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-024$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, $q$A parking garage charges $5 for the first hour and $2 for each additional hour. What is the maximum number of whole hours a driver can park without exceeding $19?$q$, $q$["8", "7", "9", "10"]$q$::jsonb, $q$8$q$, $q$5 + 2(h − 1) ≤ 19 → 2(h − 1) ≤ 14 → h − 1 ≤ 7 → h ≤ 8, so the maximum is 8 hours.$q$, $q${"7": "Solves 5 + 2h ≤ 19 without adjusting for the fact that the first hour is already covered by the flat fee, getting h ≤ 7 instead of h ≤ 8.", "9": "Rounds the boundary up instead of taking the floor of the inequality's solution, overshooting the $19 limit.", "10": "Ignores the flat-fee structure entirely and divides the full $19 by the $2 hourly rate (19 ÷ 2 = 9.5, rounded up)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-025$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, $q$What is the slope of the line given by 3x + 2y = 12?$q$, $q$["−3/2", "3/2", "2/3", "−2/3"]$q$::jsonb, $q$−3/2$q$, $q$Solving for y: 2y = −3x + 12 → y = (−3/2)x + 6, so the slope is −3/2.$q$, $q${"3/2": "Correctly finds the coefficient ratio but drops the negative sign.", "2/3": "Inverts the coefficient ratio (uses y-coefficient over x-coefficient) and drops the sign.", "−2/3": "Inverts the coefficient ratio (uses y-coefficient over x-coefficient) but keeps the correct sign."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-026$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line 2x − 5y = 20?$q$, $q$["10", "−4", "4", "−10"]$q$::jsonb, $q$10$q$, $q$Set y = 0: 2x = 20 → x = 10.$q$, $q${"−4": "Finds the y-intercept (setting x = 0) instead of the x-intercept (setting y = 0).", "4": "Finds the y-intercept but drops the negative sign in the process.", "−10": "Sets up the x-intercept calculation correctly but makes a sign error, negating the correct value of 10."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-027$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, $q$If 3x − y = 7 and x + y = 9, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["5", "4", "9", "7"]$q$::jsonb, $q$5$q$, $q$Adding the equations: 4x = 16 → x = 4. Then from x + y = 9, y = 5.$q$, $q${"4": "Solves correctly for x (x = 4) but reports it instead of continuing to solve for y.", "9": "Reports the sum x + y = 9 instead of isolating just y.", "7": "Restates the constant from the first equation instead of solving the system."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-028$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, $q$A cell phone plan costs $20 plus $0.10 per minute of calls. If a customer's bill was $35, how many minutes of calls were made?$q$, $q$["150", "15", "175", "350"]$q$::jsonb, $q$150$q$, $q$20 + 0.10m = 35 → 0.10m = 15 → m = 150.$q$, $q${"15": "Correctly isolates 0.10m = 15 but stops there, forgetting to divide by 0.10.", "175": "Uses an incorrect per-minute rate (doubling it), computing 35 ÷ 0.20 instead of properly isolating and dividing by 0.10.", "350": "Divides the full $35 bill by $0.10 without first subtracting the $20 base fee."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-029$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, $q$Company A charges $50 plus $3 per unit produced. Company B charges $20 plus $5 per unit produced. For what number of units is the total cost the same for both companies?$q$, $q$["15", "10", "30", "6"]$q$::jsonb, $q$15$q$, $q$50 + 3x = 20 + 5x → 30 = 2x → x = 15.$q$, $q${"10": "Correctly reaches 2x = 30 but divides by the wrong rate (3, from Company A) instead of the actual coefficient difference (2).", "30": "Correctly isolates 2x = 30 but stops there, forgetting the final division by 2.", "6": "Correctly reaches 2x = 30 but divides by the wrong rate (5, from Company B) instead of the actual coefficient difference (2)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-030$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −3 < 2x + 1 ≤ 7?$q$, $q$["3", "2", "−2", "−1"]$q$::jsonb, $q$3$q$, $q$−4 < 2x ≤ 6 → −2 < x ≤ 3, so the greatest integer value is 3.$q$, $q${"2": "Misapplies the inequality boundary, excluding the correct value 3 by treating '≤ 7' as a strict inequality.", "−2": "Reports the boundary from only the lower half of the compound inequality (x > −2) instead of finding the greatest value satisfying the whole inequality.", "−1": "Misapplies which end of the compound inequality to check, picking a value near the lower boundary instead of the true greatest integer solution."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-031$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 3, $q$The formula A = P(1 + rt) can be solved for r in terms of A, P, and t. Which expression is equivalent to r?$q$, $q$["(A − P)/(Pt)", "(A − Pt)/P", "A/(Pt) − 1", "(A − P)/t"]$q$::jsonb, $q$(A − P)/(Pt)$q$, $q$A = P + Prt → A − P = Prt → r = (A − P)/(Pt).$q$, $q${"(A − Pt)/P": "Distributes P incorrectly, applying it only to t rather than to the full rt term.", "A/(Pt) − 1": "Divides the entire equation by Pt before correctly isolating A − P first.", "(A − P)/t": "Forgets to divide by P as well as t, dividing only by t."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-032$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, $q$Sarah is 4 years older than twice Tom's age. If Sarah is 22 years old, how old is Tom?$q$, $q$["9", "11", "18", "13"]$q$::jsonb, $q$9$q$, $q$22 = 2t + 4 → 18 = 2t → t = 9.$q$, $q${"11": "Divides Sarah's age by 2 directly (22 ÷ 2), skipping the step of first subtracting the 4-year offset.", "18": "Correctly isolates 2t = 18 but stops there, forgetting to divide by 2.", "13": "Sign error — adds 4 instead of subtracting it, computing (22 + 4)/2."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-033$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, $q$A water tank had 80 gallons at time t = 0 and 20 gallons at t = 6 minutes, draining at a constant rate. What is the drain rate in gallons per minute?$q$, $q$["10", "60", "6", "600"]$q$::jsonb, $q$10$q$, $q$The tank lost 60 gallons over 6 minutes: 60/6 = 10 gallons per minute.$q$, $q${"60": "Reports the total gallons drained instead of dividing by the elapsed time to get a rate.", "6": "Reports the elapsed time itself instead of the computed rate.", "600": "Inverts the operation, multiplying instead of dividing the volume by the time, landing an order of magnitude too high."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-034$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 3, $q$How many solutions does the system 6x − 2y = 10 and y = 3x − 5 have?$q$, $q$["Infinitely many", "0", "Exactly 1", "Exactly 2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Infinitely many$q$, $q$Substituting: 6x − 2(3x − 5) = 10 → 6x − 6x + 10 = 10 → 10 = 10, which is always true, so the system has infinitely many solutions.$q$, $q${"0": "Assumes the two equations must represent different, non-intersecting lines without checking whether they're actually equivalent.", "Exactly 1": "Assumes a system of two linear equations always has exactly one solution by default, without recognizing these equations are equivalent.", "Exactly 2": "Miscounts, assuming a system of two lines could intersect at two points, which isn't geometrically possible."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-035$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y ≤ 3x − 2?$q$, $q$["(2, 3)", "(0, 0)", "(−1, 2)", "(1, 5)"]$q$::jsonb, $q$(2, 3)$q$, $q$For (2, 3): 3 ≤ 3(2) − 2 = 4, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when checked.$q$, $q${"(0, 0)": "Tests the default 'easy' point (0, 0) out of habit without actually checking it against the inequality (0 ≤ −2 is false).", "(−1, 2)": "Makes a sign error when substituting the negative x-value into 3x − 2.", "(1, 5)": "Flips the inequality direction while checking (treats it as y ≥ 3x − 2 instead of y ≤ 3x − 2)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-036$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, $q$A line has a slope of −3 and passes through (1, 2). What is the value of y when x = 4?$q$, $q$["−7", "−10", "7", "−1"]$q$::jsonb, $q$−7$q$, $q$y − 2 = −3(x − 1) → y = −3x + 5. At x = 4: y = −12 + 5 = −7.$q$, $q${"−10": "Uses the given point's y-coordinate (2) directly as the y-intercept instead of solving for it, computing −3(4) + 2.", "7": "Correctly computes the magnitude but drops the negative sign on the final answer.", "−1": "Inverts the point-slope operation, dividing (4 − 1) by the slope instead of multiplying by it."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-037$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, $q$Adult movie tickets cost $12 and child tickets cost $8. A group bought 5 tickets for a total of $52. How many adult tickets were purchased?$q$, $q$["3", "2", "4", "5"]$q$::jsonb, $q$3$q$, $q$Let a + c = 5 and 12a + 8c = 52. Substituting c = 5 − a: 12a + 8(5 − a) = 52 → 4a = 12 → a = 3.$q$, $q${"2": "Correctly finds the number of child tickets (c = 2) but reports it instead of the number of adult tickets asked for.", "4": "Makes an arithmetic slip while solving 4a = 12, landing on a = 4 instead of a = 3.", "5": "Reports the total number of tickets purchased instead of solving for the adult-only count."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-038$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 1, $q$If x² − 7x + 12 = 0, what is the product of the solutions?$q$, $q$["12", "7", "−12", "−7"]$q$::jsonb, $q$12$q$, $q$x² − 7x + 12 = (x − 3)(x − 4), so the roots are 3 and 4, and their product is 12.$q$, $q${"7": "Reports the sum of the solutions (3 + 4 = 7) instead of the product.", "−12": "Correctly finds the product's magnitude (12) but applies a sign error, confusing it with the sign convention for the sum.", "−7": "Reports the b-coefficient directly (−7) without applying the actual Vieta's formula for either sum or product."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-039$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 2, $q$If 2x² + 3x − 2 = 0, what is the sum of the solutions?$q$, $q$["−3/2", "3/2", "2", "−2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$−3/2$q$, $q$2x² + 3x − 2 = (2x − 1)(x + 2) = 0, so x = 1/2 or x = −2. Their sum is 1/2 + (−2) = −3/2.$q$, $q${"3/2": "Correctly computes the sum's magnitude but drops the negative sign.", "2": "Reports the magnitude of one root (from −2) instead of the sum of both roots.", "−2": "Reports one individual solution instead of computing the sum of both solutions."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-040$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 1, $q$The function f(x) = (x − 3)² + 5 has what minimum value?$q$, $q$["5", "3", "−5", "9"]$q$::jsonb, $q$5$q$, $q$In vertex form (x − h)² + k, the minimum value is k = 5, occurring at x = 3.$q$, $q${"3": "Reports the x-value at the vertex (h = 3) instead of the minimum y-value (k = 5).", "−5": "Correctly identifies the constant k = 5 but drops the sign.", "9": "Confuses the squared constant inside the vertex form (3² = 9) with the actual minimum value."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-041$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, $q$A population is modeled by P(t) = 500(1.08)^t, where t is measured in years. What is the population, to the nearest whole number, after 3 years?$q$, $q$["630", "500", "580", "700"]$q$::jsonb, $q$630$q$, $q$500(1.08)^3 = 500(1.259712) ≈ 630.$q$, $q${"500": "Reports the initial population at t = 0 without applying any growth.", "580": "Compounds the growth for only 2 years instead of 3 (500 × 1.08² ≈ 583, rounds to 580).", "700": "Overshoots by miscounting the number of compounding periods or overestimating the growth rate."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-042$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 1, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (3x²)(4x³)?$q$, $q$["12x^5", "7x^6", "12x^6", "7x^5"]$q$::jsonb, $q$12x^5$q$, $q$Multiply coefficients and add exponents: 3·4 = 12, and x²·x³ = x^5, giving 12x^5.$q$, $q${"7x^6": "Adds the coefficients instead of multiplying them (3 + 4 = 7) and multiplies the exponents instead of adding them (2 × 3 = 6).", "12x^6": "Correctly multiplies the coefficients (12) but multiplies the exponents instead of adding them (2 × 3 = 6).", "7x^5": "Adds the coefficients instead of multiplying them (3 + 4 = 7) but correctly adds the exponents (5)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-043$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 1, $q$If x² − 16 = 0, what is the positive value of x?$q$, $q$["4", "−4", "16", "8"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4$q$, $q$x² − 16 = (x − 4)(x + 4) = 0, so x = 4 or x = −4. The positive value is 4.$q$, $q${"−4": "Reports the negative solution instead of the positive value the question asks for.", "16": "Forgets to take the square root, treating x² = 16 as if x itself equals 16.", "8": "Confuses halving 16 with taking its square root, or incorrectly combines the two solutions (4 and −4)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-044$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, $q$If y = x² and y = 2x + 3, what is the sum of all values of x that satisfy both equations?$q$, $q$["2", "3", "−1", "4"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$x² = 2x + 3 → x² − 2x − 3 = 0 → (x − 3)(x + 1) = 0, so x = 3 or x = −1. Their sum is 2.$q$, $q${"3": "Reports one solution (x = 3) alone instead of the sum of both solutions.", "−1": "Reports the other solution (x = −1) alone instead of the sum of both solutions.", "4": "Miscombines the two roots with a sign error, adding 3 and 1 instead of 3 and −1."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-045$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x³)⁴ / x⁵?$q$, $q$["x^7", "x^12", "x^17", "x^2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$x^7$q$, $q$(x³)⁴ = x^12, and x^12 / x^5 = x^(12−5) = x^7.$q$, $q${"x^12": "Correctly simplifies (x³)⁴ to x^12 but forgets to divide by x^5 at all.", "x^17": "Adds the exponents instead of subtracting when dividing (12 + 5 = 17).", "x^2": "Adds instead of multiplies for the power rule (3 + 4 = 7 instead of 3 × 4 = 12), then subtracts 5 from that incorrect value."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-046$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, $q$If x² + 8x + 7 = 0, what is the sum of the solutions?$q$, $q$["−8", "−1", "−7", "8"]$q$::jsonb, $q$−8$q$, $q$Completing the square: (x + 4)² = 9 → x + 4 = ±3 → x = −1 or x = −7. Their sum is −8.$q$, $q${"−1": "Reports one solution (x = −1) alone instead of the sum of both solutions.", "−7": "Reports the other solution (x = −7) alone instead of the sum of both solutions.", "8": "Correctly finds the magnitude of the sum but drops the negative sign."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-047$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 3, $q$The polynomial f(x) = x³ − 2x² + kx − 6 has x = 2 as one of its roots. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["3", "6", "−3", "2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$3$q$, $q$f(2) = 8 − 8 + 2k − 6 = 0 → 2k − 6 = 0 → k = 3.$q$, $q${"6": "Makes a sign/arithmetic slip while simplifying f(2), double-counting a term and landing on k = 6 instead of k = 3.", "−3": "Sign error when isolating k, solving 2k = −6 instead of 2k = 6.", "2": "Reports the given root value (x = 2) itself instead of solving the equation for k."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-048$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, $q$Which expression is equivalent to x^(1/2) · x^(3/2)?$q$, $q$["x^2", "x^(3/4)", "x^(2/3)", "x"]$q$::jsonb, $q$x^2$q$, $q$Add the exponents: 1/2 + 3/2 = 2, so the expression simplifies to x².$q$, $q${"x^(3/4)": "Multiplies the exponents (1/2 × 3/2 = 3/4) instead of adding them.", "x^(2/3)": "Misapplies fraction arithmetic when combining the exponents, rather than simply adding them.", "x": "Miscombines the exponents down to 1 through a common-denominator error, landing on x^1 instead of x^2."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-049$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, $q$The height of a tossed ball is modeled by h(t) = −16t² + 64t + 5, where t is in seconds. At what time does the ball reach its maximum height?$q$, $q$["2", "4", "1", "3"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$The vertex occurs at t = −b/(2a) = −64/(2 · −16) = 2 seconds.$q$, $q${"4": "Uses −b/a instead of −b/(2a), forgetting to double the denominator (64/16 = 4).", "1": "Halves the correct answer through a stray division error in the vertex formula.", "3": "Makes an arithmetic slip while evaluating the vertex formula −b/(2a)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-050$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, $q$If f(x) = x² and g(x) = f(x − 2) + 3, what is the value of g(0)?$q$, $q$["7", "4", "1", "−1"]$q$::jsonb, $q$7$q$, $q$g(0) = f(0 − 2) + 3 = f(−2) + 3 = (−2)² + 3 = 4 + 3 = 7.$q$, $q${"4": "Correctly computes f(−2) = 4 but forgets to add the +3 shift outside the function.", "1": "Misapplies the horizontal shift, computing f(0) − 2 + 3 instead of f(0 − 2) + 3.", "−1": "Shifts in the wrong direction, treating the transformation as x + 2 instead of x − 2, producing a sign error."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-051$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 2, $q$Which expression is equivalent to (x + 3)(x − 3) + 9?$q$, $q$["x^2", "x^2 − 9", "x^2 + 9", "x^2 + 6x"]$q$::jsonb, $q$x^2$q$, $q$(x + 3)(x − 3) = x² − 9, and adding 9 gives x² − 9 + 9 = x².$q$, $q${"x^2 − 9": "Correctly expands (x + 3)(x − 3) to x² − 9 but forgets to add the +9 at the end.", "x^2 + 9": "Drops the −9 from the expanded product entirely and simply appends +9 to x².", "x^2 + 6x": "Incorrectly expands (x + 3)(x − 3) as if it were (x + 3)², producing an extra 6x term."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-052$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, $q$A radioactive sample of 80 grams halves in mass every 5 years. How many grams remain after 15 years?$q$, $q$["10", "20", "40", "5"]$q$::jsonb, $q$10$q$, $q$15 years is 3 half-lives: 80 · (1/2)³ = 80/8 = 10 grams.$q$, $q${"20": "Miscounts 15 years as 2 half-lives instead of 3 (15/5 = 3).", "40": "Miscounts 15 years as only 1 half-life.", "5": "Miscounts 15 years as 4 half-lives, one more than the actual 3."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-053$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic inequalities$q$, 3, $q$Which value of x satisfies the inequality x² − 5x + 6 < 0?$q$, $q$["2.5", "1", "4", "3.5"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2.5$q$, $q$x² − 5x + 6 = (x − 2)(x − 3) < 0 when 2 < x < 3. Only 2.5 falls in that interval.$q$, $q${"1": "Tests a value outside the true solution interval (2, 3), likely from finding where the expression is positive instead of negative.", "4": "Tests a value outside the true solution interval on the upper side, past the boundary at x = 3.", "3.5": "Tests a value just past the upper boundary (x = 3), mistakenly including it in the solution interval."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-054$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Function composition$q$, 2, $q$If f(x) = 2x + 1 and g(x) = x², what is the value of f(g(3))?$q$, $q$["19", "7", "10", "37"]$q$::jsonb, $q$19$q$, $q$g(3) = 9, so f(g(3)) = f(9) = 2(9) + 1 = 19.$q$, $q${"7": "Computes f(3) directly, forgetting to apply g(x) first before f.", "10": "Correctly computes g(3) = 9 but makes an arithmetic slip applying f to it, landing on 10 instead of 19.", "37": "Mistakenly doubles f's coefficient (using 4x + 1 instead of 2x + 1) when applying it to g(3) = 9."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-055$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Quadratic equations$q$, 3, $q$For what positive value of k does the equation x² + kx + 9 = 0 have exactly one real solution?$q$, $q$["6", "9", "3", "36"]$q$::jsonb, $q$6$q$, $q$One real solution requires the discriminant to equal 0: k² − 4(1)(9) = 0 → k² = 36 → k = 6 (taking the positive value).$q$, $q${"9": "Reports the constant term of the equation directly instead of solving for k using the discriminant.", "3": "Takes the square root of the constant term (√9 = 3) instead of correctly solving k² = 36.", "36": "Correctly sets up k² = 36 but forgets to take the square root, reporting k² itself."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-056$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Equivalent expressions$q$, 1, $q$Which expression is equivalent to √50?$q$, $q$["5√2", "10√5", "2√5", "25√2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$5√2$q$, $q$√50 = √(25 · 2) = 5√2.$q$, $q${"10√5": "Splits 50 into the factor pair (10 × 5) and mishandles which factor comes out from under the radical, also doubling the coefficient incorrectly.", "2√5": "Splits 50 into the factor pair (10 × 5) but pulls out the wrong factor as the coefficient.", "25√2": "Correctly identifies 25 as a perfect-square factor but forgets to take its square root (√25 = 5) before pulling it out."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-057$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential functions$q$, 2, $q$Which function represents exponential decay?$q$, $q$["y = 100(0.75)^x", "y = 100(1.25)^x", "y = 100 + 0.75x", "y = 0.75x^100"]$q$::jsonb, $q$y = 100(0.75)^x$q$, $q$Exponential decay has the form y = a(b)^x with 0 < b < 1. Only y = 100(0.75)^x satisfies this.$q$, $q${"y = 100(1.25)^x": "Picks a base greater than 1, which represents exponential growth, not decay.", "y = 100 + 0.75x": "Mistakes a linear function for an exponential one.", "y = 0.75x^100": "Confuses a power function (variable base, fixed exponent) with an exponential function (fixed base, variable exponent)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-058$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, $q$For the function f(x) = −2x² + 3x − 1, what happens to f(x) as x approaches infinity?$q$, $q$["f(x) approaches negative infinity", "f(x) approaches positive infinity", "f(x) approaches 0", "f(x) approaches 1"]$q$::jsonb, $q$f(x) approaches negative infinity$q$, $q$Since the leading coefficient (−2) is negative and the degree is even, the graph opens downward, so f(x) → −∞ as x → ∞.$q$, $q${"f(x) approaches positive infinity": "Ignores the negative leading coefficient, assuming all even-degree polynomials open upward.", "f(x) approaches 0": "Confuses the end behavior of a polynomial with that of a function that has a horizontal asymptote.", "f(x) approaches 1": "Mistakes the constant term for a determinant of end behavior instead of focusing on the leading term."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-059$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear systems of equations$q$, 3, $q$If y = x² − 4 and y = −x + 2, what is the greatest value of x that satisfies both equations?$q$, $q$["2", "−3", "4", "−2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$x² − 4 = −x + 2 → x² + x − 6 = 0 → (x + 3)(x − 2) = 0, so x = −3 or x = 2. The greatest value is 2.$q$, $q${"−3": "Reports the smaller root instead of the greatest value that satisfies both equations.", "4": "Makes an arithmetic slip while factoring x² + x − 6, landing on an incorrect root.", "−2": "Sign error on the correct greatest root, reporting −2 instead of 2."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-060$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Nonlinear functions$q$, 2, $q$The function y = x² − 6x + 5 has what minimum value?$q$, $q$["−4", "5", "−9", "4"]$q$::jsonb, $q$−4$q$, $q$Completing the square: y = (x − 3)² − 9 + 5 = (x − 3)² − 4, so the minimum value is −4.$q$, $q${"5": "Reports the constant term of the original expression instead of completing the square.", "−9": "Reports the intermediate constant from completing the square (−9) before adding back the +5.", "4": "Correctly finds the magnitude of the minimum but drops the negative sign."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-061$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Exponential equations$q$, 2, $q$If 3^(2x) = 81, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["2", "4", "1.5", "8"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$81 = 3^4, so 2x = 4 → x = 2.$q$, $q${"4": "Correctly solves 2x = 4 (since 81 = 3⁴) but forgets to divide by 2 for the final value of x.", "1.5": "Miscounts the power of 3 needed to reach 81, throwing off the final division.", "8": "Doubles the correct answer, multiplying instead of dividing by 2 at the final step."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-062$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Advanced Math$q$, $q$Polynomial functions$q$, 1, $q$If f(x) = x³ − 3x, what is the value of f(−1)?$q$, $q$["2", "−2", "4", "−4"]$q$::jsonb, $q$2$q$, $q$f(−1) = (−1)³ − 3(−1) = −1 + 3 = 2.$q$, $q${"−2": "Combines two sign errors — mishandles the cube's sign and drops the sign on the −3 term — computing 1 − 3 = −2.", "4": "Treats (−1)³ as if the negative sign disappears, computing (+1) − 3(−1) = 1 + 3 = 4.", "−4": "Correctly cubes −1 but drops the sign when distributing the −3, computing (−1) − 3 = −4."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-063$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, $q$A car travels 270 miles using 9 gallons of gas. What is the car's fuel efficiency, in miles per gallon?$q$, $q$["30", "27", "9", "270"]$q$::jsonb, $q$30$q$, $q$270 miles ÷ 9 gallons = 30 miles per gallon.$q$, $q${"27": "Miscalculates the division, perhaps rounding the gallons figure before dividing instead of using 9 exactly.", "9": "Reports the gallons value itself instead of computing miles per gallon.", "270": "Reports the total miles driven instead of computing the rate."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-064$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, $q$In a class of 32 students, the ratio of boys to girls is 3:5. How many girls are in the class?$q$, $q$["20", "12", "15", "17"]$q$::jsonb, $q$20$q$, $q$The ratio has 8 total parts. Girls = (5/8) · 32 = 20.$q$, $q${"12": "Correctly computes the boys count (3/8 × 32 = 12) but reports it instead of the girls count the question asks for.", "15": "Miscalculates the total number of ratio parts, throwing off the fraction applied to 32.", "17": "Subtracts an incorrectly calculated boys count from 32, compounding an earlier ratio error."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-065$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, $q$A stock rose in value from $40 to $52. What was the percent increase?$q$, $q$["30%", "12%", "23%", "33%"]$q$::jsonb, $q$30%$q$, $q$Percent increase = (52 − 40)/40 × 100 = 12/40 × 100 = 30%.$q$, $q${"12%": "Reports the raw dollar increase ($12) directly as a percentage, without dividing by the original price.", "23%": "Divides the increase by the new value ($52) instead of the original value ($40) as the base.", "33%": "Divides the increase by an incorrectly averaged or reduced base instead of the original price."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-066$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, $q$What is 20% of 60% of 500?$q$, $q$["60", "100", "120", "50"]$q$::jsonb, $q$60$q$, $q$60% of 500 = 300. 20% of 300 = 60.$q$, $q${"100": "Applies 20% directly to 500, skipping the required first step of finding 60% of 500.", "120": "Miscalculates 60% of 500 as 600 (misplacing a decimal) before taking 20% of that incorrect value.", "50": "Incorrectly combines the two percentages (e.g., averaging or misapplying them) instead of applying them sequentially."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-067$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, $q$A data set is 3, 5, 5, 7, 9, 50. Which measure of center is most affected by the value 50?$q$, $q$["The mean", "The median", "The mode", "Neither the mean nor the median"]$q$::jsonb, $q$The mean$q$, $q$The mean is sensitive to extreme values (outliers), while the median, based on position, changes little when one extreme value is present.$q$, $q${"The median": "Assumes the median is equally sensitive to outliers as the mean, without recognizing its resistance to extreme values.", "The mode": "Mistakes the most frequent value for a measure that would shift due to a single extreme value.", "Neither the mean nor the median": "Assumes outliers don't meaningfully affect any measure of center, missing the mean's known sensitivity."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-068$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, $q$Which of the following data sets has the greatest standard deviation?$q$, $q$["{1, 10, 10, 19}", "{10, 10, 10, 10}", "{5, 10, 10, 15}", "{9, 10, 10, 11}"]$q$::jsonb, $q${1, 10, 10, 19}$q$, $q$Standard deviation measures spread from the mean. {1, 10, 10, 19} has the widest spread of values around its mean, giving it the greatest standard deviation.$q$, $q${"{10, 10, 10, 10}": "Confuses the magnitude of the numbers with their spread — this set actually has zero standard deviation.", "{5, 10, 10, 15}": "Has real spread but less than the correct set; likely chosen by only loosely eyeballing the range instead of comparing spread from the mean.", "{9, 10, 10, 11}": "Has the least spread among the varying sets; likely chosen by not comparing the actual distances from each set's mean."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-069$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, $q$A scatterplot of study hours versus test score has a line of best fit given by y = 5x + 60, where x is hours studied and y is the test score. What does the slope of this line represent?$q$, $q$["Each additional hour of study is associated with an increase of 5 points on the test.", "A student who studies 0 hours is predicted to score 5 points.", "Each additional point on the test requires 5 more hours of study.", "The maximum possible test score is 5."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Each additional hour of study is associated with an increase of 5 points on the test.$q$, $q$In a linear model y = mx + b, the slope m represents the change in y per unit increase in x — here, 5 additional points per additional hour studied.$q$, $q${"A student who studies 0 hours is predicted to score 5 points.": "Mistakes the slope (5) for the y-intercept (60), misreading which coefficient represents the baseline score at x = 0.", "Each additional point on the test requires 5 more hours of study.": "Inverts the relationship between x and y, treating the slope as hours-per-point instead of points-per-hour.", "The maximum possible test score is 5.": "Misreads the slope coefficient as an upper bound on the score rather than a rate of change."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-070$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 1, $q$A bag contains 4 red, 3 blue, and 5 green marbles. What is the probability of randomly drawing a blue marble?$q$, $q$["1/4", "1/3", "3/5", "1/12"]$q$::jsonb, $q$1/4$q$, $q$There are 12 total marbles, and 3 are blue, so the probability is 3/12 = 1/4.$q$, $q${"1/3": "Computes the probability of drawing a red marble (4/12) instead of a blue one.", "3/5": "Compares the blue count only to one other color's count instead of the total number of marbles.", "1/12": "Treats only a single blue marble as favorable instead of all 3 blue marbles."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-071$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Conditional probability$q$, 3, $q$Of 200 students, 120 play sports; of those, 80 also play an instrument. Of the 80 students who don't play sports, 50 play an instrument. What is the probability that a randomly selected student who plays an instrument also plays sports?$q$, $q$["8/13", "80/200", "80/120", "50/130"]$q$::jsonb, $q$8/13$q$, $q$Total instrument players = 80 + 50 = 130. Of those, 80 also play sports, so the probability is 80/130 = 8/13.$q$, $q${"80/200": "Uses the total number of students as the denominator instead of the total number of instrument players.", "80/120": "Uses the total number of sports players as the denominator, confusing which group should form the base of this conditional probability.", "50/130": "Uses the count of instrument players who don't play sports as the numerator instead of those who play both."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-072$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Inference from sample statistics$q$, 2, $q$A poll of 400 randomly selected voters found that 220 favor a proposal. Based on this sample, what is the best estimate of the percentage of all voters who favor the proposal?$q$, $q$["55%", "220%", "400%", "45%"]$q$::jsonb, $q$55%$q$, $q$220/400 = 0.55 = 55%.$q$, $q${"220%": "Reports the raw count of favorable responses as if it were already a percentage.", "400%": "Reports the total sample size as if it were a percentage.", "45%": "Computes the complement — the percentage who do NOT favor the proposal — instead of the percentage who do."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-073$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, $q$Which change would most directly decrease the margin of error of a survey estimate?$q$, $q$["Increasing the sample size", "Decreasing the sample size", "Asking a more biased question", "Surveying fewer people from the population"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Increasing the sample size$q$, $q$Margin of error generally decreases as sample size increases, because larger samples produce more precise estimates of the population.$q$, $q${"Decreasing the sample size": "Reverses the actual relationship between sample size and margin of error.", "Asking a more biased question": "Confuses margin of error (a measure of precision) with bias (a measure of accuracy) — a different statistical concept.", "Surveying fewer people from the population": "Repeats the same reversal as decreasing sample size, treating a smaller sample as more precise."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-074$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, $q$A student scored 80 on a test worth 30% of the final grade and 90 on a test worth 70% of the final grade. What is the student's weighted average?$q$, $q$["87", "85", "88", "86"]$q$::jsonb, $q$87$q$, $q$0.30(80) + 0.70(90) = 24 + 63 = 87.$q$, $q${"85": "Computes a simple, unweighted average of the two scores instead of applying the given weights.", "88": "Arithmetic slip computing 0.70 × 90 as 64 instead of 63.", "86": "Arithmetic slip computing 0.30 × 80 as 23 instead of 24."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-075$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 1, $q$A printer prints 45 pages in 3 minutes at a constant rate. How many pages will it print in 20 minutes?$q$, $q$["300", "45", "225", "60"]$q$::jsonb, $q$300$q$, $q$Rate = 45/3 = 15 pages per minute. In 20 minutes: 15 × 20 = 300 pages.$q$, $q${"45": "Reports the given 3-minute total instead of scaling it up to 20 minutes.", "225": "Miscalculates the per-minute rate and multiplies incorrectly when scaling to 20 minutes.", "60": "Miscalculates the rate as pages per some other unit (e.g., 3 pages per minute) instead of the correct 15 pages per minute."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-076$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, $q$A recipe that serves 4 people requires 250 grams of flour. How many grams of flour are needed to serve 10 people at the same ratio?$q$, $q$["625", "600", "1000", "400"]$q$::jsonb, $q$625$q$, $q$250 × (10/4) = 250 × 2.5 = 625 grams.$q$, $q${"600": "Rounds the per-person amount (62.5 g) down to 60 g before scaling up to 10 people, instead of keeping the exact value.", "1000": "Mistakenly multiplies by 4 (the original serving size) instead of the correct scale factor of 2.5.", "400": "Applies the scaling in the wrong direction, as if serving more people required less flour."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-077$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, $q$A study concludes that 'most people exercise regularly' based on a survey of visitors to a gym. What is the main flaw in this conclusion?$q$, $q$["The sample is not representative of the general population.", "The sample size is too large to be useful.", "Gym visitors cannot be surveyed accurately.", "Exercise habits cannot be measured through surveys."]$q$::jsonb, $q$The sample is not representative of the general population.$q$, $q$Surveying only gym visitors overrepresents people who already exercise, so the sample cannot be generalized to the population as a whole.$q$, $q${"The sample size is too large to be useful.": "Misidentifies the flaw as being about the quantity of respondents rather than who was sampled.", "Gym visitors cannot be surveyed accurately.": "Introduces an unrelated measurement-validity concern instead of the actual sampling bias.", "Exercise habits cannot be measured through surveys.": "Overgeneralizes to a claim about surveys in general, missing the specific representativeness issue."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-078$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 3, $q$A jar contains 5 red and 7 blue candies. Two candies are drawn at random without replacement. What is the probability that both are red?$q$, $q$["5/33", "25/144", "5/12", "4/11"]$q$::jsonb, $q$5/33$q$, $q$P(both red) = (5/12) × (4/11) = 20/132 = 5/33.$q$, $q${"25/144": "Fails to account for 'without replacement,' treating the two draws as independent with the same probability each time (5/12 × 5/12).", "5/12": "Reports the probability of just the first draw instead of both draws combined.", "4/11": "Reports the probability of just the second draw (after one red is already removed) instead of the combined probability."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-079$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 2, $q$After a 20% discount, a shirt costs $32. What was the shirt's original price?$q$, $q$["$40", "$38.40", "$44", "$36"]$q$::jsonb, $q$$40$q$, $q$If the original price is p, then 0.80p = 32 → p = 40.$q$, $q${"$38.40": "Increases the discounted price by 20% directly (32 × 1.2) instead of solving 0.80p = 32 for the original price.", "$44": "Adds a miscalculated flat dollar amount to the sale price instead of solving the percentage equation.", "$36": "Adds a smaller miscalculated flat amount to the sale price instead of solving the percentage equation."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-080$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Two-variable data$q$, 2, $q$In a scatterplot with a line of best fit, a data point lies far above the line. What does this indicate about that data point?$q$, $q$["Its actual value is greater than the value predicted by the line.", "Its actual value is less than the value predicted by the line.", "It lies exactly on the line of best fit.", "It cannot be used to evaluate the model."]$q$::jsonb, $q$Its actual value is greater than the value predicted by the line.$q$, $q$A point above the line of best fit has an actual y-value greater than the model's predicted value at that x.$q$, $q${"Its actual value is less than the value predicted by the line.": "Reverses the direction, confusing a point 'above' the line with one 'below' it.", "It lies exactly on the line of best fit.": "Contradicts the premise that the point lies far above the line.", "It cannot be used to evaluate the model.": "Introduces an unsupported claim about usability instead of correctly interpreting the residual's direction."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-081$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Ratios and proportions$q$, 1, $q$An alloy is 40% copper by mass. How many kilograms of copper are in 25 kilograms of the alloy?$q$, $q$["10", "15", "4", "40"]$q$::jsonb, $q$10$q$, $q$0.40 × 25 = 10 kilograms.$q$, $q${"15": "Miscalculates 40% of 25, computing something closer to 60% of 25 instead.", "4": "Confuses which number is the total, applying the percentage to the wrong base value.", "40": "Reports the percentage value itself (40) instead of applying it to the 25 kg total."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-082$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Probability$q$, 2, $q$A fair coin is flipped 3 times. What is the probability of getting at least one heads?$q$, $q$["7/8", "1/8", "3/8", "1/2"]$q$::jsonb, $q$7/8$q$, $q$P(at least one heads) = 1 − P(no heads) = 1 − (1/2)³ = 1 − 1/8 = 7/8.$q$, $q${"1/8": "Computes the probability of getting no heads at all, forgetting the final complement step (1 − 1/8).", "3/8": "Computes the probability of getting exactly one heads instead of at least one.", "1/2": "Applies the single-flip probability to the whole three-flip scenario, ignoring how the probabilities compound."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-083$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 1, $q$A data set has a mean of 70 and a standard deviation of 5. What value is 2 standard deviations above the mean?$q$, $q$["80", "75", "72", "85"]$q$::jsonb, $q$80$q$, $q$70 + 2(5) = 70 + 10 = 80.$q$, $q${"75": "Adds only 1 standard deviation instead of 2 (70 + 5).", "72": "Adds only a fraction of one standard deviation, an arithmetic slip in scaling.", "85": "Adds 3 standard deviations instead of 2 (70 + 15)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-084$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Percentages$q$, 1, $q$In a survey, 60 of 150 respondents preferred Option A. What percent of respondents preferred a different option?$q$, $q$["60%", "40%", "90%", "30%"]$q$::jsonb, $q$60%$q$, $q$150 − 60 = 90 respondents preferred another option. 90/150 = 0.60 = 60%.$q$, $q${"40%": "Computes the percentage who preferred Option A (60/150) instead of those who preferred a different option.", "90%": "Reports the raw count of other respondents (90) as if it were already a percentage.", "30%": "Uses an incorrect, doubled denominator (90/300) instead of the correct total of 150."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-085$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$One-variable data$q$, 2, $q$A distribution of household incomes is strongly right-skewed, with a small number of very high incomes. Which is most likely true of the mean and median?$q$, $q$["The mean is greater than the median.", "The median is greater than the mean.", "The mean and median are equal.", "The relationship cannot be determined."]$q$::jsonb, $q$The mean is greater than the median.$q$, $q$In a right-skewed distribution, a few very high values pull the mean upward more than they affect the median, so the mean exceeds the median.$q$, $q${"The median is greater than the mean.": "Reverses the actual relationship between mean and median in a right-skewed distribution.", "The mean and median are equal.": "Assumes skewness has no effect on the relationship between mean and median, missing the core concept.", "The relationship cannot be determined.": "Fails to apply the general rule connecting skew direction to the mean-median relationship."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-086$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Rates and units$q$, 2, $q$A car travels at 90 kilometers per hour. What is this speed in meters per second?$q$, $q$["25", "15", "30", "90"]$q$::jsonb, $q$25$q$, $q$90 km/h = 90,000 m / 3,600 s = 25 m/s.$q$, $q${"15": "Divides by an incorrect conversion factor (e.g., 6) instead of the correct 3.6.", "30": "Uses an incorrect conversion factor of 3 instead of 3.6 (90 ÷ 3 = 30).", "90": "Forgets to convert units at all, reporting the km/h value directly as m/s."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-087$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$q$, $q$Evaluating statistical claims$q$, 2, $q$A company wants to estimate the average commute time for all of its employees. Which sampling method would produce the most reliable estimate?$q$, $q$["Randomly selecting 200 employees from the full employee roster", "Asking only employees who arrive earliest each day", "Surveying employees who volunteer to respond", "Asking only employees in the company's headquarters office"]$q$::jsonb, $q$Randomly selecting 200 employees from the full employee roster$q$, $q$A random sample drawn from the entire employee roster is representative of the whole population, unlike the biased methods in the other choices.$q$, $q${"Asking only employees who arrive earliest each day": "Introduces bias by systematically excluding employees with longer or later commutes.", "Surveying employees who volunteer to respond": "Introduces self-selection bias, since volunteers may not represent the typical employee.", "Asking only employees in the company's headquarters office": "Excludes employees at other locations, who may have very different commute times."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-088$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 1, $q$Two angles of a triangle measure 55° and 65°. What is the measure of the third angle?$q$, $q$["60°", "70°", "55°", "65°"]$q$::jsonb, $q$60°$q$, $q$The angles of a triangle sum to 180°: 180 − 55 − 65 = 60°.$q$, $q${"70°": "Arithmetic slip while subtracting 55 and 65 from 180.", "55°": "Repeats one of the given angles instead of solving for the missing third angle.", "65°": "Repeats the other given angle instead of solving for the missing third angle."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-089$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 1, $q$A triangle with sides 3, 4, and 5 is similar to a second triangle whose shortest side is 9. What is the length of the second triangle's longest side?$q$, $q$["15", "12", "20", "9"]$q$::jsonb, $q$15$q$, $q$The scale factor is 9/3 = 3, so the longest side is 5 × 3 = 15.$q$, $q${"12": "Applies the correct scale factor (3) but to the wrong side of the original triangle (the middle side, 4, instead of the longest, 5).", "20": "Miscalculates the scale factor (using 4 instead of 3) before applying it to the longest side.", "9": "Reports the given shortest side of the second triangle instead of solving for its longest side."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-090$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, $q$A triangle has a base of 10 and a height of 6. What is its area?$q$, $q$["30", "60", "16", "20"]$q$::jsonb, $q$30$q$, $q$Area = (1/2)(base)(height) = (1/2)(10)(6) = 30.$q$, $q${"60": "Forgets to multiply by 1/2 in the triangle area formula, computing base × height directly.", "16": "Adds the base and height instead of applying the area formula.", "20": "Miscalculates using an incorrect partial formula instead of (1/2)(base)(height)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-091$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, $q$A circle has a radius of 5. What is its area?$q$, $q$["25π", "10π", "5π", "50π"]$q$::jsonb, $q$25π$q$, $q$Area = πr² = π(5²) = 25π.$q$, $q${"10π": "Computes the circumference (2πr) instead of the area.", "5π": "Uses r instead of r² in the area formula.", "50π": "Doubles the correct area, perhaps by using 2r² instead of r²."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-092$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, $q$A circle has a radius of 12. What is the length of an arc with a central angle of 60°?$q$, $q$["4π", "2π", "6π", "12π"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4π$q$, $q$Arc length = (60/360)(2π)(12) = (1/6)(24π) = 4π.$q$, $q${"2π": "Uses a central angle of 30° instead of the given 60° (fraction 1/12 instead of 1/6).", "6π": "Uses a central angle of 90° instead of the given 60° (fraction 1/4 instead of 1/6).", "12π": "Uses a central angle of 180° instead of the given 60° (fraction 1/2 instead of 1/6), or skips the angle fraction and just halves the circumference."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-093$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, $q$A rectangular prism has dimensions 4, 5, and 6. What is its volume?$q$, $q$["120", "60", "90", "100"]$q$::jsonb, $q$120$q$, $q$Volume = 4 × 5 × 6 = 120.$q$, $q${"60": "Applies an extra 1/2 factor, as if computing a triangular prism or pyramid volume instead of a rectangular prism.", "90": "Substitutes an incorrect value for one dimension before multiplying (e.g., 5 × 6 × 3 instead of 4 × 5 × 6).", "100": "Misreads one dimension as 5 instead of 6, computing 4 × 5 × 5 instead of 4 × 5 × 6."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-094$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 2, $q$In a right triangle, the side opposite angle θ has length 8, and the hypotenuse has length 17. What is sin(θ)?$q$, $q$["8/17", "15/17", "8/15", "15/8"]$q$::jsonb, $q$8/17$q$, $q$sin(θ) = opposite/hypotenuse = 8/17.$q$, $q${"15/17": "Computes cos(θ) (adjacent/hypotenuse) instead of sin(θ) (opposite/hypotenuse).", "8/15": "Computes tan(θ) (opposite/adjacent) instead of sin(θ) (opposite/hypotenuse).", "15/8": "Computes cot(θ) (adjacent/opposite) instead of sin(θ) (opposite/hypotenuse)."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-095$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 1, $q$A right triangle has legs of length 9 and 12. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["15", "21", "13", "225"]$q$::jsonb, $q$15$q$, $q$√(9² + 12²) = √(81 + 144) = √225 = 15.$q$, $q${"21": "Adds the two legs directly instead of applying the Pythagorean theorem.", "13": "Recalls a different, memorized Pythagorean triple (5-12-13) instead of computing this triangle's actual hypotenuse.", "225": "Forgets to take the square root, reporting 9² + 12² = 225 directly."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-096$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 2, $q$A cylinder has a radius of 3 and a height of 10. What is its volume?$q$, $q$["90π", "30π", "60π", "9π"]$q$::jsonb, $q$90π$q$, $q$Volume = πr²h = π(3²)(10) = 90π.$q$, $q${"30π": "Forgets to square the radius, computing π × r × h instead of π × r² × h.", "60π": "Doubles the radius instead of squaring it (2 × 3 × 10 × π).", "9π": "Computes only the base area (πr²) without multiplying by the height."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-097$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Lines and angles$q$, 2, $q$Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle formed measures 70°. What is the measure of its same-side (co-interior) angle?$q$, $q$["110°", "70°", "90°", "20°"]$q$::jsonb, $q$110°$q$, $q$Co-interior angles are supplementary: 180 − 70 = 110°.$q$, $q${"70°": "Assumes co-interior angles are equal, like alternate interior angles, instead of supplementary.", "90°": "Defaults to assuming the two angles must be complementary (summing to 90°) rather than supplementary.", "20°": "Computes 90 − 70 = 20, confusing a complementary relationship with the correct supplementary one."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-098$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 2, $q$If sin(θ) = 3/5 for an acute angle θ, what is cos(θ)?$q$, $q$["4/5", "3/4", "5/3", "5/4"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4/5$q$, $q$This is a 3-4-5 right triangle: with opposite = 3 and hypotenuse = 5, the adjacent side is 4, so cos(θ) = 4/5.$q$, $q${"3/4": "Confuses cos(θ) with tan(θ), reporting opposite/adjacent instead of adjacent/hypotenuse.", "5/3": "Inverts the sine ratio (hypotenuse/opposite) instead of computing cosine.", "5/4": "Inverts the correct cosine ratio (hypotenuse/adjacent) instead of adjacent/hypotenuse."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-099$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, $q$A circle is given by the equation (x − 2)² + (y + 3)² = 16. What is its radius?$q$, $q$["4", "16", "2", "8"]$q$::jsonb, $q$4$q$, $q$The equation is in the form (x − h)² + (y − k)² = r², so r² = 16 and r = 4.$q$, $q${"16": "Reports r² directly instead of taking the square root to find r.", "2": "Miscalculates the square root of 16.", "8": "Doubles the correct radius instead of taking the square root of 16 correctly."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-100$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, $q$A circle has a radius of 6. What is the area of a sector with a central angle of 90°?$q$, $q$["9π", "36π", "18π", "3π"]$q$::jsonb, $q$9π$q$, $q$Sector area = (90/360)(π)(6²) = (1/4)(36π) = 9π.$q$, $q${"36π": "Forgets to apply the 90/360 fraction, reporting the full circle's area instead of the sector's.", "18π": "Uses an incorrect fraction (180/360 = 1/2) instead of the correct 90/360 = 1/4.", "3π": "Uses an incorrect, too-small fraction (30/360 = 1/12) instead of the correct 90/360 = 1/4."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-101$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 2, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle, the shorter leg has length 5. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["10", "5√3", "5√2", "15"]$q$::jsonb, $q$10$q$, $q$In a 30-60-90 triangle, the hypotenuse is twice the shorter leg: 2 × 5 = 10.$q$, $q${"5√3": "Mistakes the longer-leg formula (short leg × √3) for the hypotenuse formula.", "5√2": "Applies the 45-45-90 triangle's √2 relationship instead of the 30-60-90 triangle's ×2 relationship.", "15": "Uses an incorrect multiplier (×3) for the hypotenuse instead of the correct ×2."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-102$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Special right triangles$q$, 1, $q$In a 45-45-90 triangle, each leg has length 7. What is the length of the hypotenuse?$q$, $q$["7√2", "14", "7√3", "49"]$q$::jsonb, $q$7√2$q$, $q$In a 45-45-90 triangle, the hypotenuse equals leg × √2, so the hypotenuse is 7√2.$q$, $q${"14": "Doubles the leg directly instead of applying the √2 factor characteristic of 45-45-90 triangles.", "7√3": "Applies the 30-60-90 triangle's √3 relationship instead of the 45-45-90 triangle's √2 relationship.", "49": "Squares the leg length instead of applying the correct hypotenuse formula."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-103$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Triangle angles$q$, 2, $q$A triangle's exterior angle at one vertex equals the sum of the two remote interior angles, which measure 50° and 65°. What is the measure of the exterior angle?$q$, $q$["115°", "65°", "50°", "180°"]$q$::jsonb, $q$115°$q$, $q$Exterior angle = 50 + 65 = 115°.$q$, $q${"65°": "Reports one remote interior angle alone instead of the sum of both.", "50°": "Reports the other remote interior angle alone instead of the sum of both.", "180°": "Assumes the exterior angle must relate to the full triangle's angle sum rather than just the two remote interior angles."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-104$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 1, $q$A circle has a circumference of 18π. What is its diameter?$q$, $q$["18", "9", "36", "6π"]$q$::jsonb, $q$18$q$, $q$Circumference = πd → 18π = πd → d = 18.$q$, $q${"9": "Reports the radius instead of the diameter.", "36": "Doubles the correct diameter, likely by re-applying a ×2 step that was already accounted for.", "6π": "Fails to fully solve for a numeric diameter, leaving π in the final answer."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-105$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 2, $q$A cone has a radius of 3 and a height of 8. What is its volume?$q$, $q$["24π", "72π", "216π", "8π"]$q$::jsonb, $q$24π$q$, $q$Volume = (1/3)πr²h = (1/3)π(9)(8) = 24π.$q$, $q${"72π": "Forgets to apply the 1/3 factor for cone volume, computing πr²h as if it were a cylinder.", "216π": "Uses r³ instead of r² in the volume formula, as if computing a different solid's volume.", "8π": "Divides the correct volume by an extra factor of 3, over-applying the 1/3 term."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-106$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangles$q$, 2, $q$A 13-foot ladder leans against a wall with its base 5 feet from the wall. How high up the wall does the ladder reach?$q$, $q$["12", "14", "10", "144"]$q$::jsonb, $q$12$q$, $q$√(13² − 5²) = √(169 − 25) = √144 = 12 feet.$q$, $q${"14": "Misapplies the Pythagorean relationship, perhaps adding the given lengths instead of properly combining their squares.", "10": "Recalls a different, memorized right-triangle ratio (6-8-10) instead of computing this triangle's actual height.", "144": "Forgets to take the square root of 169 − 25 = 144, reporting the squared value directly."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-107$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Similar triangles$q$, 2, $q$A tree casts a 24-foot shadow at the same time a 6-foot person casts an 8-foot shadow. How tall is the tree?$q$, $q$["18", "32", "20", "16"]$q$::jsonb, $q$18$q$, $q$Using similar triangles: 6/8 = h/24 → h = 6 × (24/8) = 18 feet.$q$, $q${"32": "Inverts the similar-triangles ratio, using 8/6 instead of 6/8 as the scale factor.", "20": "Miscombines the given lengths instead of correctly setting up the proportion between the two triangles.", "16": "Mismatches which shadow length corresponds to which height when setting up the proportion."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-108$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 1, $q$A cube has edge length 5. What is its surface area?$q$, $q$["150", "125", "100", "25"]$q$::jsonb, $q$150$q$, $q$Surface area = 6(edge²) = 6(25) = 150.$q$, $q${"125": "Computes the cube's volume (5³) instead of its surface area.", "100": "Forgets to include all 6 faces, multiplying the face area by 4 instead of 6.", "25": "Reports the area of a single face only, forgetting to multiply by the number of faces."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-109$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Right triangle trigonometry$q$, 3, $q$If sin(x°) = cos(30°) and x is an acute angle, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["60", "30", "45", "90"]$q$::jsonb, $q$60$q$, $q$Since sin(x°) = cos(90° − x°), setting 90 − x = 30 gives x = 60.$q$, $q${"30": "Reports the given angle (30°) directly instead of applying the sine-cosine co-function identity.", "45": "Assumes the two angles must simply average out, without applying the actual complementary-angle identity.", "90": "Misapplies the complementary angle relationship, assuming sin and cos are only ever equal at 90°."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-110$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Circles$q$, 2, $q$An inscribed angle intercepts an arc measuring 100°. What is the measure of the inscribed angle?$q$, $q$["50°", "100°", "25°", "200°"]$q$::jsonb, $q$50°$q$, $q$An inscribed angle equals half the measure of its intercepted arc: 100/2 = 50°.$q$, $q${"100°": "Reports the arc measure directly instead of halving it to find the inscribed angle.", "25°": "Incorrectly quarters the arc measure instead of halving it.", "200°": "Doubles the arc measure instead of halving it."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-111$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Coordinate geometry$q$, 1, $q$What is the distance between the points (1, 2) and (4, 6)?$q$, $q$["5", "7", "25", "√13"]$q$::jsonb, $q$5$q$, $q$Distance = √[(4−1)² + (6−2)²] = √(9 + 16) = √25 = 5.$q$, $q${"7": "Adds the coordinate differences directly instead of applying the distance formula.", "25": "Forgets to take the square root, reporting 3² + 4² = 25 directly.", "√13": "Miscalculates one of the coordinate differences before squaring and summing."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$),
  ($q$M-112$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Geometry and Trigonometry$q$, $q$Area and volume$q$, 3, $q$A cube has a volume of 27. If each side length is doubled, what is the volume of the new cube?$q$, $q$["216", "54", "108", "729"]$q$::jsonb, $q$216$q$, $q$The original edge length is 3 (since 3³ = 27). Doubling gives an edge of 6, so the new volume is 6³ = 216.$q$, $q${"54": "Doubles the original volume (27 × 2) instead of cubing the doubled edge length.", "108": "Quadruples the original volume (27 × 4) instead of applying the correct 2³ = 8 scale factor.", "729": "Confuses the new edge length, computing 9³ instead of correctly finding and cubing the doubled edge, 6."}$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$published$q$)
;
